ForgeData.register("bus",{"BUS-1":{"label":"Marketing & People","color":"#b45309","questions":[{"id":"BUS-01","stem":"A business moves from a product-orientated approach to a market-orientated approach. Which best describes the difference?","options":{"A":"Product orientation develops goods based on what the business can make","B":"Product orientation focuses on price; market orientation focuses on quality.","C":"Market orientation only applies to services, not manufactured goods.","D":"There is no practical difference between the two approaches."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Product orientation = 'we make it, they'll buy it' (Ford Model T — any colour as long as it's black). Market orientation = research what customers want, then produce it. Market orientation requires continuous market research, is more customer-led, more responsive to change but costlier to maintain. Edexcel examiners flag students who describe these as the same or confuse them with niche vs mass market.","reforge":{"stem":"Apple's development of the iPhone involved extensive consumer research into what people wanted from a handheld device before designing the product. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Product orientation — Apple designed what they thought was best.","B":"Niche marketing — Apple only targeted one segment.","C":"Market orientation — Apple researched consumer needs first","D":"Mass marketing — Apple sold to everyone."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-02","stem":"A business has fixed costs of £200,000, a selling price of £50 per unit, and variable costs of £30 per unit. What is the break-even output?","options":{"A":"4,000 units","B":"10,000 units","C":"6,667 units","D":"40,000 units"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even = Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution per unit. Contribution = Selling price − Variable cost = £50 − £30 = £20. Break-even = £200,000 ÷ £20 = 10,000 units. Common errors: dividing by price instead of contribution, or forgetting to subtract variable costs first. Always show the contribution step.","reforge":{"stem":"Fixed costs rise to £240,000. Selling price and variable costs stay the same. What is the new break-even output?","options":{"A":"Profit measures success over time — large firms report it every quarter.","B":"4,800 units","C":"8,000 units","D":"12,000 units (£240,000 ÷ £20 contribution)"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-03","stem":"A student writes: 'A price skimming strategy means the business sets the lowest possible price.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Demand is price inelastic — the quantity fall was smaller than the price rise.","B":"Price skimming only applies to supermarkets.","C":"Price skimming sets a HIGH initial price to target early adopters.","D":"Price skimming and penetration pricing are the same strategy."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"Skimming = high price initially → targets early adopters willing to pay a premium (e.g. new iPhone launches) → price falls as competition increases. Penetration = low price initially → gains market share quickly → price rises once established. Students frequently swap these. Remember: 'skim the cream off the top' = start high.","reforge":{"stem":"Netflix entered the UK market in 2012 with a low monthly subscription price to attract subscribers quickly. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Penetration pricing — low initial price to rapidly gain market share before raising prices later.","B":"Price skimming — high initial price for early adopters.","C":"Predatory pricing — illegally undercutting competitors.","D":"Hygiene factors motivate workers and motivators prevent dissatisfaction (this is Herzberg reversed)."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-04","stem":"Which of the following is a difference between a sole trader and a private limited company (Ltd)?","options":{"A":"Sole traders must have at least two owners.","B":"Avoiding all uncertainty by closing the business","C":"Only Ltd companies can make a profit.","D":"A private limited company has limited liability"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Key distinctions: Sole trader — unlimited liability (personal assets at risk), simple to set up, all profit kept, no public accounts. Ltd — limited liability (shareholders protected), must file accounts at Companies House, shares can only be sold privately (not on stock exchange), more complex admin. Unlimited vs limited liability is the most-tested distinction.","reforge":{"stem":"A sole trader's business fails with £80,000 in debts. The owner has personal savings of £50,000 and a car worth £15,000. What is the maximum the creditors can claim?","options":{"A":"£80,000 — but only from business assets.","B":"£80,000 — from both business and personal assets, because unlimited liability means personal assets are not protected.","C":"£50,000 — only from personal savings.","D":"The supplier must wait until the business becomes profitable before any debt can be repaid, since a sole trader's personal assets are legally ring-fenced from business creditors."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-05","stem":"Herzberg's two-factor theory distinguishes between hygiene factors and motivators. Which correctly identifies each?","options":{"A":"Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators actively drive performance.","B":"Hygiene factors motivate workers and motivators prevent dissatisfaction (this is Herzberg reversed).","C":"Hygiene factors and motivators are the same concept in different words.","D":"Motivators are only relevant to managers, not shop floor workers."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Herzberg: hygiene factors (salary, job security, working conditions, company policy) — adequate = no dissatisfaction, inadequate = dissatisfaction. They do NOT motivate. Motivators (achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement) — presence = motivation and job satisfaction. Implication: paying workers more stops them being unhappy but doesn't make them work harder. Contrast with Taylor (money motivates) and Maslow (hierarchy of needs).","reforge":{"stem":"A company improves its canteen and increases salaries. According to Herzberg, what is the likely effect on staff motivation?","options":{"A":"Motivation will increase significantly — better pay and facilities make staff work harder.","B":"No effect — Herzberg said nothing about pay.","C":"Dissatisfaction may fall, but motivation will not necessarily rise","D":"Motivation will fall because staff become complacent."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-06","stem":"Ansoff's Matrix shows four growth strategies. A business selling its existing product to a new market is pursuing:","options":{"A":"Market penetration","B":"Market development","C":"Product development","D":"Diversification"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Ansoff Matrix: Market penetration = existing product, existing market (lowest risk). Market development = existing product, NEW market (e.g. expanding abroad). Product development = NEW product, existing market. Diversification = new product, new market (highest risk). Students frequently swap market development and product development. The key question: is the product new or existing?","reforge":{"stem":"McDonald's launches a new plant-based burger to sell to its existing customers in its existing restaurants. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Market development — new market targeted.","B":"Deleting the product immediately to free up resources for new development.","C":"Diversification — new product and new market.","D":"Product development — new product sold to the existing market."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-07","stem":"A business has revenue of £500,000 and total costs of £420,000. What is the net profit margin?","options":{"A":"84%","B":"80,000%","C":"16%","D":"19%"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Net profit margin = (Net profit ÷ Revenue) × 100 = (£80,000 ÷ £500,000) × 100 = 16%. Net profit = Revenue − Total costs = £500,000 − £420,000 = £80,000. Common errors: dividing profit by costs instead of revenue, or confusing gross profit margin (which uses gross profit, not net profit).","reforge":{"stem":"Revenue = £800,000. Cost of sales = £300,000. Other costs = £200,000. What is the gross profit margin?","options":{"A":"62.5% (gross profit = £800k − £300k = £500k; margin = £500k ÷ £800k × 100)","B":"Gross 16%, operating 40% — operating profit is always higher than gross profit.","C":"50%","D":"25%"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-08","stem":"Evaluate whether price is always the most important element of the marketing mix for a business. (12 marks)","type":"extended_answer","model_answer_outline":{"KAA_strand_1":"Price affects demand directly via PED — for price-elastic goods (close substitutes, luxury items), competitive pricing is critical. A price above competitors loses sales quickly. Apply: budget airlines (Ryanair) where price dominates buying decisions.","KAA_strand_2":"However, for price-inelastic goods (necessities, strong brands), product quality and brand image (promotion) may matter more. Apply: Apple — customers pay premium prices because of perceived product superiority and brand loyalty, not because Apple has the lowest price.","EV_strand":"Depends on: (1) market type — commodity markets (petrol, wheat) compete mainly on price; differentiated markets compete on brand, quality, service; (2) target segment — price-sensitive consumers (budget shoppers) vs quality-conscious consumers; (3) product life cycle — price more important at maturity (competition peaks) than at launch (skimming strategy). Judgement: price is one of four interdependent elements — for most businesses, the optimal marketing mix balances all four, with price's relative importance varying by context."},"tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"12-mark Business essays: AO1 (knowledge), AO2 (application to context/business), AO3 (evaluation — weigh the argument, consider counter-evidence, reach a judgement). Use specific businesses as examples, not generic descriptions.","spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T1-01","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A market-oriented firm is most likely to begin with:","options":{"A":"customer needs and market research","B":"the production method already owned","C":"the directors' preferred product","D":"the lowest possible wage"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Market orientation starts with customer needs and evidence about demand.","reforge":{"stem":"A café surveys customers before designing a new menu. Which approach is it using?","options":{"A":"Financial gearing, because it uses a survey","B":"Production orientation, because it ignores demand","C":"Market orientation, because it starts with customer needs","D":"Product orientation focuses on price; market orientation focuses on quality."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-02","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"Which is a benefit of segmenting a market?","options":{"A":"identical advertising for every buyer","B":"a more focused marketing offer","C":"removing all competitors","D":"making demand perfectly certain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Segmentation helps a firm tailor its product and promotion to a defined group.","reforge":{"stem":"A sportswear company creates separate offers for runners and hikers. What is the main benefit?","options":{"A":"It makes demand perfectly predictable","B":"It removes the need for market research","C":"Market orientation only applies to services, not manufactured goods.","D":"It can target each segment with a more relevant marketing mix"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-03","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A brand is best described as:","options":{"A":"There is no practical difference between the two approaches.","B":"a legal form of ownership","C":"a recognised identity linked to a product","D":"the number of units sold"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Branding creates recognition and associations that can affect perceived value.","reforge":{"stem":"Customers recognise a distinctive name, logo and set of associations when choosing a product. This is the firm's:","options":{"A":"Brand identity","B":"Variable cost","C":"Legal structure","D":"Distribution channel"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-04","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"Primary market research involves:","options":{"A":"Product orientation — Apple designed what they thought was best.","B":"using a government statistic unchanged","C":"estimating last year's profit","D":"collecting new data for the specific decision"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Primary research is gathered first-hand for the firm's current purpose.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm asks 500 potential customers how often they would buy a proposed product. This is:","options":{"A":"Secondary research using published statistics","B":"Primary market research","C":"A cash-flow forecast","D":"A balance-sheet calculation"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.2"},{"id":"BUS-T1-05","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A focus group is mainly useful for exploring:","options":{"A":"detailed opinions about a proposed product","B":"Niche marketing — Apple only targeted one segment.","C":"the firm's tax liability","D":"the depreciation charge"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Discussion can reveal attitudes and reactions, although the sample may not be representative.","reforge":{"stem":"A small group discusses its reactions to an advert while a researcher moderates. What method is this?","options":{"A":"A profitability ratio","B":"A census of the whole market","C":"A focus group","D":"A production forecast"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-06","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"If a product has elastic demand, a price cut is likely to:","options":{"A":"reduce quantity demanded","B":"increase total revenue if quantity rises proportionately more","C":"Price discrimination — charging different customers different prices.","D":"raise the average cost automatically"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"With elastic demand, the percentage rise in quantity exceeds the percentage fall in price.","reforge":{"stem":"If a 10% price cut causes quantity demanded to rise by 20%, what happens to total revenue?","options":{"A":"It becomes unrelated to quantity sold","B":"It isn't — price skimming does mean setting the lowest price.","C":"It stays unchanged in every market","D":"It is likely to rise because demand is price elastic"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-07","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"Penetration pricing is intended to:","options":{"A":"recover development costs immediately with a high price","B":"sell only to a luxury segment","C":"gain market share with a relatively low launch price","D":"avoid any promotional spending"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"A low initial price can encourage trial and rapid adoption.","reforge":{"stem":"A new streaming service launches with a low monthly price to attract subscribers quickly. Which strategy is this?","options":{"A":"Penetration pricing","B":"Price skimming","C":"Cost-plus pricing","D":"Predatory purchasing"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-08","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A distribution channel describes:","options":{"A":"Production orientation, because it ignores demand","B":"the level of employee motivation","C":"the size of the firm's overdraft","D":"the route a product takes to reach the customer"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Channels may involve direct selling, retailers, wholesalers or digital platforms.","reforge":{"stem":"A manufacturer sells directly through its own website rather than through wholesalers. This changes its:","options":{"A":"Market share calculation","B":"Distribution channel","C":"Employee appraisal system","D":"Capital structure"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-09","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A unique selling point gives a product:","options":{"A":"a distinctive benefit competitors do not match easily","B":"a guaranteed monopoly","C":"Segmentation is a legal requirement for all consumer-facing firms under UK marketing law.","D":"an exemption from consumer law"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"A USP differentiates the offer; it does not guarantee market dominance.","reforge":{"stem":"A detergent claims it removes stains more effectively than rival products. This claim is intended to be its:","options":{"A":"Mass marketing — Apple sold to everyone.","B":"Current liability","C":"Unique selling point","D":"Economy of scale"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-10","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"Which is a likely advantage of e-commerce for a small firm?","options":{"A":"Financial gearing, because it uses a survey","B":"access to customers beyond its local area","C":"guaranteed repeat purchases","D":"zero marketing costs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Online selling can widen reach, but fulfilment and promotion still create costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A local craft firm begins selling online and receives orders from other regions. What benefit has it gained?","options":{"A":"Automatic control of competitors' prices","B":"Guaranteed repeat purchases","C":"Zero fulfilment costs","D":"Access to a wider market"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T1-11","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"An entrepreneur is most directly responsible for:","options":{"A":"Nothing — the debt is written off when a business fails.","B":"guaranteeing every employee a job","C":"organising resources while accepting business risk","D":"auditing all government accounts"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Entrepreneurs combine resources and make decisions under uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur opens a business and commits personal funds despite uncertainty about demand. What role is shown?","options":{"A":"Organising resources while accepting business risk","B":"Setting the national interest rate","C":"Auditing the government's accounts","D":"Being required to publish accounts for the first time."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-T1-12","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A drawback of a sole trader is:","options":{"A":"automatic access to public share capital","B":"separation of owner and business identity","C":"no control over day-to-day decisions","D":"unlimited liability for business debts"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"The owner and business are not legally separate, so personal assets may be at risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A sole trader's business cannot repay a supplier. Which consequence reflects unlimited liability?","options":{"A":"Only the firm's shares can ever be lost","B":"The owner's personal assets may be used to repay business debts","C":"Hygiene factors and motivators are the same concept in different words.","D":"The debt disappears when sales fall"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-T1-13","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"Limited liability means shareholders generally:","options":{"A":"risk only the capital invested in the company","B":"The government automatically pays the supplier","C":"cannot lose money on shares","D":"are responsible for every company debt"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"A company is a separate legal entity, subject to exceptions such as wrongful conduct.","reforge":{"stem":"What protection does limited liability normally give shareholders in a company?","options":{"A":"Their shares can never lose value","B":"Price skimming and penetration pricing are the same strategy.","C":"Their loss is generally limited to the capital they invested","D":"They personally guarantee every company debt"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-T1-14","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A stakeholder is:","options":{"A":"Slower growth, while employees demand rapid expansion.","B":"a person or group affected by business decisions","C":"a customer who has complained","D":"a government department only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Stakeholders include employees, customers, suppliers, owners, government and communities.","reforge":{"stem":"A founder spots an unmet customer need and invests savings in testing a new product. Which entrepreneurial behaviour is shown?","options":{"A":"Managing the national interest rate","B":"Copying a rival without making a decision","C":"A company with widely dispersed institutional shareholders.","D":"Identifying an opportunity and accepting calculated risk"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-T1-15","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A conflict between employees and shareholders may arise because employees may prefer:","options":{"A":"lower job security and lower wages","B":"Nothing — the debt is written off when a business fails.","C":"higher wages while shareholders seek higher dividends","D":"more pollution near the workplace"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Stakeholders can value different outcomes from the same decision.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager asks employees for views before choosing a new rota. Which leadership style is this?","options":{"A":"Democratic leadership","B":"Autocratic leadership","C":"Laissez-faire accounting","D":"Paternalistic ownership"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-T1-16","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A social enterprise primarily aims to:","options":{"A":"Motivation will increase significantly — better pay and facilities make staff work harder.","B":"avoid measuring its performance","C":"operate only as a government department","D":"meet a social or environmental purpose while remaining financially viable"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Surpluses are commonly reinvested to support the social mission.","reforge":{"stem":"A start-up's founder tests several ideas before committing funds. Which quality is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Guaranteed certainty","B":"Calculated risk-taking","C":"Avoidance of opportunity","D":"Complete dependence on competitors"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-T1-17","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"An ethical sourcing policy is most likely to affect:","options":{"A":"supplier choice, costs and brand reputation","B":"only the firm's depreciation method","C":"the legal definition of GDP","D":"Sole traders pay more tax than Ltd companies."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Ethical choices can raise input costs but strengthen trust and reduce reputational risk.","reforge":{"stem":"Employees help choose a workplace change and then show stronger commitment to implementing it. What is a likely benefit of consultation?","options":{"A":"A guaranteed fall in operating costs","B":"Removal of all decision-making responsibility","C":"Greater employee commitment","D":"An end to the need for management"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-LEADERS-01","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"An entrepreneur is most likely to be characterised by:","options":{"A":"A company with widely dispersed institutional shareholders.","B":"identifying an opportunity and accepting calculated risk","C":"avoiding all change in the business","D":"working without any business objective"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Entrepreneurs identify opportunities, organise resources and accept uncertainty when pursuing an idea.","reforge":{"stem":"A founder launches a product after researching demand but accepts that sales are uncertain. Which role is illustrated?","options":{"A":"Competitor price fixing","B":"Motivators are only relevant to managers, not shop floor workers.","C":"Central-bank policy setting","D":"Entrepreneurial opportunity recognition and calculated risk"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-LEADERS-02","spec":"BUS-1","stem":"A democratic leadership style involves:","options":{"A":"giving orders without seeking views","B":"delegating every decision to customers","C":"consulting employees before important decisions","D":"Lower prices, while employees want higher prices."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Consultation can improve commitment, although it may make decisions slower.","reforge":{"stem":"A team leader invites staff to discuss alternatives before making a decision. This is closest to:","options":{"A":"Democratic leadership","B":"Autocratic leadership","C":"Hands-off leadership with no accountability","D":"Management by financial ratio"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-N1-01","stem":"A firm using primary research rather than secondary research is:","options":{"A":"Using last year's sales figures from its own records.","B":"Relying on government statistics already published.","C":"Reading a competitor's annual report for cost data.","D":"Gathering new data for its own specific purpose"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Primary research is collected first-hand for the decision at hand — surveys, interviews, focus groups, observation, test marketing. It is current and specific but costs more and takes longer. Secondary research uses data gathered by someone else or previously by the firm — government statistics, trade journals, competitor accounts, internal sales records. It is cheap and quick but may be dated, less relevant, or collected for a different purpose.","reforge":{"stem":"A start-up with a very limited budget needs an overview of market size quickly. It should begin with:","options":{"A":"A programme of in-depth customer interviews.","B":"Secondary research, which is cheaper and faster","C":"Test marketing the product in three regions.","D":"A national survey of several thousand consumers."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-02","stem":"A firm uses a quota sample rather than a random sample. This means respondents are:","options":{"A":"Selected to match set proportions of each group","B":"Chosen entirely at random from the population.","C":"Limited to existing customers of the business.","D":"Interviewed only once the product has launched."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Random sampling gives every member of the population an equal chance of selection, so results can be generalised with known confidence, but it is costly and slow. Quota sampling fills preset proportions — say 50% male, 30% aged under 25 — so it is quicker and cheaper and ensures each group appears, but selection within each quota is not random, so bias can enter. Larger samples reduce sampling error but raise cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Increasing sample size from 100 to 1,000 respondents mainly affects the research by:","options":{"A":"Removing the need to identify a target market.","B":"Guaranteeing the findings will be correct.","C":"Reducing sampling error, at greater cost","D":"Eliminating all forms of interviewer bias."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-03","stem":"Market segmentation involves dividing a market into groups of buyers who:","options":{"A":"Are located in the same geographical region only.","B":"Share similar characteristics or needs","C":"Have identical incomes and occupations.","D":"Have already purchased the firm's product."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Segments can be defined demographically (age, gender, income, occupation), geographically, psychographically (attitudes, lifestyle, values) or behaviourally (usage rate, loyalty, benefits sought). Segmenting lets a firm tailor the marketing mix, target promotion efficiently and often charge more to a group that values the offer. The costs are a smaller addressable market per segment and higher complexity in production and promotion.","reforge":{"stem":"A car manufacturer targets buyers who value environmental responsibility above cost. This segmentation is:","options":{"A":"Geographic, based on where buyers live.","B":"Behavioural, based on purchase frequency.","C":"Demographic, based on age and income.","D":"Psychographic, based on values"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-N1-04","stem":"A product with a high market share in a low-growth market is classified on the Boston Matrix as a:","options":{"A":"Problem child, needing heavy investment to grow.","B":"Star, requiring investment to defend its position.","C":"Cash cow, generating funds for other products","D":"Dog, with weak share in a weak market."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Boston Matrix by share and market growth: Star — high share, high growth, needs investment but promises future returns. Cash cow — high share, low growth, generates surplus cash to fund others. Problem child (question mark) — low share, high growth, needs investment with uncertain outcome. Dog — low share, low growth, usually divested. A balanced portfolio uses cash cows to fund stars and selected problem children.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a firm want cash cows in its portfolio even though their markets are barely growing?","options":{"A":"They generate cash to fund others","B":"They require the largest marketing budgets.","C":"They always have the highest profit margins.","D":"They guarantee future growth in market share."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-N1-05","stem":"A product entering the decline stage of its life cycle would typically show:","options":{"A":"Sales stable at their peak level.","B":"Rapidly rising sales and growing market share.","C":"Its highest promotional spending of any stage.","D":"Falling sales, prompting extension or exit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Life cycle stages: development (costs, no sales), introduction (low sales, heavy promotion, often losses), growth (rising sales, profits emerge, competitors enter), maturity (peak sales, intense competition, price pressure), decline (falling sales). Extension strategies — new markets, new packaging, product updates, price cuts — prolong maturity. The cycle explains cash-flow patterns and why a firm needs products at different stages.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action is an extension strategy rather than a new product launch?","options":{"A":"Closing the production line and exiting the market.","B":"Reformulating and repackaging the existing product","C":"Developing an entirely different product category.","D":"Selling the brand name to a competitor."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-06","stem":"A firm sets price below cost temporarily to drive rivals from the market. This is:","options":{"A":"Predatory pricing, likely unlawful","B":"Price skimming, charging a high launch price.","C":"Cost-plus pricing, adding a margin to unit cost.","D":"Psychological pricing, ending prices at .99."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"Pricing strategies: skimming — high launch price for innovative products, recovering development costs from early adopters. Penetration — low price to win share quickly. Cost-plus — unit cost plus a margin, simple but ignores demand. Competitive — matching rivals. Psychological — £9.99. Predatory — below cost to eliminate competitors, which is unlawful in the UK and EU. Price discrimination charges different groups different prices for the same product.","reforge":{"stem":"A rail operator charges more at peak times than off-peak for the same journey. This is:","options":{"A":"Predatory pricing aimed at rival operators.","B":"Penetration pricing to build market share.","C":"Price discrimination between customer groups","D":"Cost-plus pricing based on the journey cost."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.2"},{"id":"BUS-N1-07","stem":"If demand for a product is price inelastic, a price increase will:","options":{"A":"Reduce total revenue because sales fall sharply.","B":"Increase total revenue, as quantity falls less","C":"Leave total revenue completely unchanged.","D":"Increase the quantity demanded by consumers."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"Price elasticity of demand = % change in quantity ÷ % change in price. Inelastic (between 0 and −1): quantity responds less than proportionately, so raising price raises revenue — typical of necessities, addictive goods and strong brands with few substitutes. Elastic (below −1): quantity responds more than proportionately, so raising price cuts revenue. Elasticity is influenced by substitutes, necessity, brand loyalty and the time available to adjust.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor makes demand for a product more price elastic?","options":{"A":"The product taking a very small share of income.","B":"Strong brand loyalty among regular buyers.","C":"The product being an essential necessity.","D":"The availability of many close substitutes"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-N1-08","stem":"A firm's income elasticity of demand is measured as +2.5. Its product is:","options":{"A":"An inferior good, bought less as income rises.","B":"A necessity with weak response to income.","C":"A luxury, demand outpacing income","D":"A good with no relationship to income at all."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"Income elasticity = % change in quantity ÷ % change in income. Positive means a normal good; above +1 marks a luxury, where demand rises faster than income and sales are highly sensitive to the economic cycle. Between 0 and +1 is a necessity, relatively stable through a downturn. A negative figure indicates an inferior good, bought more as incomes fall — value ranges and budget travel are examples.","reforge":{"stem":"During a recession, which type of product would a firm expect to see rising demand for?","options":{"A":"Inferior goods, with negative income elasticity","B":"Goods with income elasticity close to zero.","C":"Luxury goods with high positive income elasticity.","D":"Products with perfectly inelastic demand."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-N1-09","stem":"A firm adopting a niche marketing strategy rather than mass marketing accepts:","options":{"A":"Greater reliance on economies of scale.","B":"Lower prices in return for very high volume.","C":"Higher production costs but a wider customer base.","D":"A smaller market, but less competition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Mass marketing targets the whole market with one mix: high volume, economies of scale, lower unit costs, but intense competition and thin margins. Niche marketing serves a small distinct segment: less competition, stronger customer loyalty and often a price premium, but limited volume, fewer scale economies, and vulnerability if that segment's tastes shift or a larger rival enters. Neither is inherently better; it depends on the firm's resources and market.","reforge":{"stem":"A key risk of relying on a single market niche is that the firm:","options":{"A":"Cannot charge a premium price for its product.","B":"Is exposed if that segment's demand falls","C":"Is unable to build any customer loyalty.","D":"Must always compete on cost with large rivals."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-10","stem":"Adding value means a business:","options":{"A":"Raises the selling price above the cost of inputs","B":"Increases the quantity of raw materials purchased.","C":"Reduces the number of stages in production.","D":"Lowers its selling price to attract more buyers."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Added value is the difference between the price charged and the cost of bought-in inputs. It can be created through branding, design, quality, convenience, speed of service, customer experience or after-sales support. Higher added value supports better margins and some insulation from price competition. Note it is not the same as profit, which is what remains after all costs including labour and overheads are met.","reforge":{"stem":"A coffee shop charges £3.50 for a coffee whose ingredients cost £0.40. The added value comes mainly from:","options":{"A":"The wholesale cost of the coffee beans.","B":"The tax paid on the final sale price.","C":"Branding, service and convenience","D":"The quantity of coffee sold each day."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-11","stem":"A business with a flat organisational structure rather than a tall one typically has:","options":{"A":"A very narrow span of control at every level.","B":"Wider spans of control and fewer layers","C":"More layers of management between top and bottom.","D":"Slower communication from the top downwards."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"A tall structure has many layers and narrow spans of control: close supervision and clear promotion paths, but slow communication, higher management costs and less delegation. A flat structure has few layers and wide spans: faster communication, lower overheads and more delegation, which can motivate staff, but managers may be stretched and supervision is lighter. Delayering removes layers to cut cost and speed decisions, at some risk to workload and morale.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm delayers its management structure. A likely drawback is that remaining managers face:","options":{"A":"Narrower spans of control than before.","B":"Reduced authority to make any decisions.","C":"Slower communication with the shop floor.","D":"Increased workload for managers"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-12","stem":"According to Herzberg, improving pay and working conditions will mainly:","options":{"A":"Motivate staff to increase their productivity.","B":"Satisfy self-actualisation needs at the top of the hierarchy.","C":"Prevent dissatisfaction rather than create motivation","D":"Create job enrichment through greater responsibility."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Herzberg separates hygiene factors — pay, conditions, supervision, company policy, job security — which cause dissatisfaction when poor but do not motivate when good, from motivators — achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement, the work itself — which do. The practical implication is job enrichment: giving more challenging and complete tasks. Contrast with Taylor, who saw pay as the prime motivator, and Maslow's hierarchy of needs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action best reflects Herzberg's motivators rather than hygiene factors?","options":{"A":"Giving responsibility for a whole task","B":"Improving the staff canteen and rest areas.","C":"Introducing a small annual pay increase.","D":"Revising the company's disciplinary policy."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-N1-13","stem":"A high labour turnover rate is most likely to increase a firm's:","options":{"A":"Overall labour productivity.","B":"Level of accumulated staff experience.","C":"Long-term employee loyalty.","D":"Recruitment and training costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Labour turnover = staff leaving ÷ average number employed × 100. High turnover raises recruitment, selection and induction costs, loses accumulated know-how, disrupts teams and can damage service quality. Some turnover is healthy, bringing new ideas. Causes include poor pay relative to rivals, weak management, limited progression, and a buoyant local labour market. Retention responses include better reward, training, progression routes and job design.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's labour turnover is far above the industry average. The most useful first step is to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the training budget to cut costs.","B":"Investigate why employees leave","C":"Recruit only temporary staff in future.","D":"Increase output targets for remaining staff."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-N1-14","stem":"Which measure shows how much output each employee produces?","options":{"A":"Output per employee over a period","B":"Total revenue divided by the number of employees.","C":"Total wage cost divided by units produced.","D":"Employees leaving divided by employees hired."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Labour productivity = total output ÷ number of employees. Raising it lowers unit labour cost and improves competitiveness, and can be achieved through training, better equipment, improved motivation, or reorganising the production process. Distinguish it from labour cost per unit, which also depends on wage rates: a highly paid but very productive workforce can still be cheaper per unit than a low-paid, low-productivity one.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's wages rise 10% while output per worker rises 25%. The labour cost per unit will:","options":{"A":"Rise, because wages have increased.","B":"Stay exactly the same as before.","C":"Fall, as productivity rose more","D":"Become impossible to calculate."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-N1-15","stem":"A firm choosing to outsource part of its recruitment process is most likely seeking:","options":{"A":"Complete control over every hiring decision.","B":"Specialist expertise at lower fixed cost","C":"To increase the size of its own HR department.","D":"To avoid complying with employment legislation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Outsourcing hands an activity to an external provider, converting fixed costs into variable ones and giving access to specialist skills and capacity. Risks include reduced control over quality, dependence on the supplier, possible loss of internal expertise, and reputational exposure if the provider behaves badly. Legal duties such as employment and data protection law remain with the firm; they cannot be outsourced along with the task.","reforge":{"stem":"A significant risk of outsourcing customer service is that the firm:","options":{"A":"Must permanently increase its own headcount.","B":"Becomes exempt from consumer protection law.","C":"Converts variable costs into fixed costs.","D":"Loses direct control over service quality"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-N1-16","stem":"A private limited company differs from a public limited company because a private company:","options":{"A":"Has unlimited liability for its shareholders.","B":"Is not required to keep any financial records.","C":"Cannot sell shares to the general public","D":"Must be owned by a single individual."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Both private (Ltd) and public (plc) companies are separate legal entities with limited liability and must file accounts. A private company cannot offer shares to the public, so ownership stays closed and control is easier to retain, but raising large sums is harder. A plc can list and raise capital from the public, gaining access to finance and profile, at the cost of dilution, greater disclosure, takeover risk and short-term market pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"A family firm converts from Ltd to plc. The most likely drawback is:","options":{"A":"Diluted ownership and possible loss of control","B":"Becoming unable to pay dividends to owners.","C":"Being required to publish accounts for the first time.","D":"Losing limited liability protection entirely."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-17","stem":"A conflict between shareholders and employees typically arises because shareholders prioritise:","options":{"A":"Slower growth, while employees demand rapid expansion.","B":"Lower prices, while employees want higher prices.","C":"Environmental performance over all financial returns.","D":"Returns, while employees seek pay"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Stakeholders include shareholders, employees, customers, suppliers, government, local communities and pressure groups. Their objectives conflict: dividends versus wages, low prices versus supplier margins, expansion versus local environmental impact, short-term profit versus long-term investment. Stakeholder mapping by power and interest helps a firm decide whom to manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed or simply monitor.","reforge":{"stem":"A stakeholder with high power but low interest in a decision should generally be:","options":{"A":"Ignored until the decision is implemented.","B":"Kept satisfied, to avoid obstruction","C":"Managed closely with detailed daily involvement.","D":"Given full control over the final decision."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-N1-18","stem":"A social enterprise differs from a conventional company because it primarily:","options":{"A":"Reinvests surpluses for a social aim","B":"Avoids paying any tax on its trading activity.","C":"Operates only with volunteer, unpaid labour.","D":"Is prohibited from generating any surplus."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"A social enterprise trades commercially but exists to achieve a social or environmental purpose, reinvesting most surplus rather than distributing it to shareholders. It must still be financially viable, pay staff and meet its obligations. Distinguish it from a charity, which is constituted differently and relies more on donations, and from corporate social responsibility, which is a conventional firm's voluntary conduct alongside its profit objective.","reforge":{"stem":"A profit-seeking firm publishes a sustainability report and cuts packaging waste. This is best described as:","options":{"A":"Becoming a registered social enterprise.","B":"Converting into a charitable organisation.","C":"Corporate social responsibility","D":"A legal requirement under company law."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-N1-19","stem":"A SMART objective differs from a general aim because it is:","options":{"A":"Concerned only with financial performance.","B":"Specific and measurable with a time limit","C":"Set by employees rather than by managers.","D":"Guaranteed to be achieved within the year."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"An aim is a broad long-term intention; objectives translate it into targets. SMART objectives are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Time-bound, which makes progress reviewable and responsibility clear. Corporate objectives cascade into functional objectives for marketing, finance, operations and HR. Poorly set objectives — vague, unrealistic or conflicting between functions — undermine motivation and make performance impossible to judge.","reforge":{"stem":"Which objective is SMART rather than a general aim?","options":{"A":"To become the best-known brand in the industry.","B":"To grow the business as much as possible.","C":"To improve customer satisfaction over time.","D":"To increase market share by 5% within 12 months"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-20","stem":"In a SWOT analysis, a new competitor entering the market would be recorded as:","options":{"A":"A strength, since it validates the market.","B":"A weakness in the firm's internal resources.","C":"A threat: external and unfavourable","D":"An opportunity to increase total market size."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"SWOT separates internal factors the firm controls — strengths and weaknesses such as brand, skills, finance, capacity — from external factors it does not: opportunities and threats such as competitors, regulation, technology and economic conditions. The commonest error is classifying an external development as a weakness. SWOT is a summary tool: its value lies in the strategy drawn from it, not in the list itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's over-reliance on one major customer is best recorded in a SWOT analysis as:","options":{"A":"A weakness: internal and unfavourable","B":"A threat, because the customer is external.","C":"An opportunity to deepen the relationship.","D":"A strength, because sales are guaranteed."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N1-21","stem":"A decision tree helps managers by:","options":{"A":"Replacing the need for any market research.","B":"Removing all uncertainty from the decision.","C":"Guaranteeing the most profitable outcome occurs.","D":"Quantifying expected values of options"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"A decision tree sets out options, chance events with estimated probabilities, and financial outcomes, then works backwards computing expected values: EV = Σ(probability × outcome), less the cost of the option. It forces explicit assumptions and comparison. Limitations: probabilities are estimates and often subjective, it ignores qualitative factors such as staff morale and reputation, and it can give false confidence in a single number.","reforge":{"stem":"An option has a 60% chance of £100,000 profit and a 40% chance of £50,000 loss. Its expected value is:","options":{"A":"£50,000, taking the average of the two outcomes.","B":"£40,000, from £60,000 less £20,000","C":"£150,000, adding the two possible outcomes.","D":"£100,000, taking the most likely outcome."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-N1-22","stem":"Ansoff's matrix describes selling an existing product into a new market as:","options":{"A":"Market development","B":"Product development for existing customers.","C":"Market penetration within the current market.","D":"Diversification into unrelated activity."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Ansoff's matrix by product and market: market penetration — existing product, existing market, lowest risk, achieved through promotion, loyalty schemes or price. Market development — existing product, new market, such as exporting or a new segment. Product development — new product, existing market. Diversification — new product, new market, the highest risk since neither is familiar, though it spreads risk across activities.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK bakery begins exporting its existing range to France. On Ansoff's matrix this is:","options":{"A":"Diversification, since France is unfamiliar.","B":"Product development for the same customers.","C":"Market development into a new market","D":"Market penetration in the current market."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N5-01","stem":"A firm's market share is calculated as its:","options":{"A":"Profit divided by the capital it employs.","B":"Sales as a share of total market sales","C":"Revenue divided by the number of competitors.","D":"Growth rate compared with the previous year."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Market share = firm's sales ÷ total market sales × 100, by value or by volume. Rising share suggests the firm is outperforming rivals; a firm can grow sales yet lose share if the whole market is growing faster. Share matters because it indicates competitive position and bargaining power with suppliers and retailers, and often correlates with scale economies. Market size and growth are separate measures and should not be confused with it.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's sales rise 5% while the whole market grows 12%. Its market share has:","options":{"A":"Become impossible to determine.","B":"Risen, since sales increased.","C":"Stayed exactly the same as before.","D":"Fallen, since rivals grew faster"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"}]},"BUS-2":{"label":"Finance & Marketing Decisions","color":"#0e7490","questions":[{"id":"BUS-09","stem":"A firm raises its price by 10% and revenue FALLS. What does this indicate about price elasticity of demand for its product?","options":{"A":"Demand is price elastic (|PED| > 1) — the quantity fall exceeded the price rise, so total revenue fell.","B":"Demand is price inelastic — the quantity fall was smaller than the price rise.","C":"Demand is perfectly inelastic — quantity did not respond at all to the price change.","D":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"Revenue test: price ↑ + revenue ↑ → inelastic. Price ↑ + revenue ↓ → elastic. Price ↑ + revenue unchanged → unit elastic. Determinants of PED: availability of substitutes (biggest), necessity vs luxury, brand loyalty, proportion of income, time horizon. Strategy link: inelastic demand supports premium pricing; elastic demand rewards competitive pricing and differentiation to REDUCE elasticity. Numerical answers should quote %ΔQd / %ΔP.","reforge":{"stem":"A business wants to make demand for its product LESS price elastic over time. Which action most directly achieves this?","options":{"A":"Price skimming and penetration pricing are the same strategy, which confuses two opposite pricing approaches with each other entirely.","B":"Increasing output capacity.","C":"Building brand loyalty and differentiation so customers see fewer acceptable substitutes.","D":"Switching to cheaper raw materials."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-10","stem":"A profitable business fails because it cannot pay suppliers on time. What concept explains how a business can be profitable yet fail?","options":{"A":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.","B":"Cash flow and profit differ: cash pays bills, profit does not — a firm can be profitable yet insolvent.","C":"Diseconomies of scale eventually erode profitable businesses.","D":"The business must have been miscounting revenue as profit."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Profit = revenue − costs over a period (accruals basis). Cash flow = timing of money actually moving. Causes of profitable-but-cash-poor: long debtor collection periods, overtrading (growing too fast), stockpiling, seasonal gaps, capital expenditure. Remedies: negotiate longer payables/shorter receivables, debt factoring, overdraft, JIT stock. Exam mantra: 'cash pays the bills, profit does not' — more businesses fail from cash-flow problems than from lack of profitability.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action improves a firm's short-term cash position WITHOUT necessarily changing its profit?","options":{"A":"Raising selling prices across the range.","B":"Increasing advertising to boost sales.","C":"Cutting the wage bill by making staff redundant.","D":"Negotiating 60-day supplier terms instead of 30"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-11","stem":"A firm's break-even output is 8,000 units and it currently sells 10,000. Its margin of safety is:","options":{"A":"Diseconomies of scale eventually erode profitable businesses.","B":"8,000 units — the break-even level itself.","C":"2,000 units — current output minus break-even output","D":"25% of fixed costs."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Margin of safety = actual (or budgeted) output − break-even output. Here 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000 units, or 20% of current sales. Interpretation: the cushion against demand shocks — a small margin signals vulnerability. Linked calculations: break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit; contribution = price − variable cost per unit. Evaluation angle: break-even analysis assumes constant price and costs and that everything produced is sold — limitations worth one evaluative paragraph.","reforge":{"stem":"If fixed costs RISE and everything else stays the same, what happens to break-even output and the margin of safety?","options":{"A":"Break-even output rises (more units needed to cover higher fixed costs), so the margin of safety shrinks.","B":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.","C":"Break-even falls; margin of safety rises.","D":"Break-even rises; margin of safety also rises."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-12","stem":"Why do businesses segment their markets rather than treating all customers identically?","options":{"A":"Segmentation is a legal requirement for all consumer-facing firms under UK marketing law.","B":"Segmentation reduces the total size of the addressable market to a manageable level.","C":"Segmentation is designed to guarantee that every possible customer purchases the product.","D":"Segmentation lets firms target distinct groups with a tailored mix"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Segmentation bases: demographic (age, income), geographic, psychographic (lifestyle, values), behavioural (usage, loyalty). Benefits: targeted marketing mix, higher conversion per pound spent, niche opportunities, price discrimination between segments. Risks: segments too small to be profitable, higher costs from multiple mixes, cannibalisation. Link chain for analysis marks: segment identified → mix tailored → customer need met better → sales/margin impact.","reforge":{"stem":"A rail company charges more at 8am than at 11am for the same journey. Which segmentation base and pricing logic is this?","options":{"A":"Geographic segmentation with cost-plus pricing.","B":"Behavioural segmentation with price discrimination","C":"Psychographic segmentation with skimming.","D":"Demographic segmentation with penetration pricing."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-13","stem":"In the Boston Matrix, a product with HIGH market share in a LOW-growth market is classified as a:","options":{"A":"Cash cow — it generates strong, steady cash with little investment need","B":"Star — high share always means star status.","C":"Diversification — new product and new market, requiring heavy investment with no guarantee of the steady cash a high-share, low-growth product already provides.","D":"Dog — low growth makes any product a dog."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Boston Matrix axes: market GROWTH (not product growth) and RELATIVE market share. Star: high share/high growth — invest to hold. Cash cow: high share/low growth — milk it. Question mark: low share/high growth — invest selectively or divest. Dog: low share/low growth — divest or harvest. Classic error: confusing share with growth, and assuming dogs must always be dropped (they may support the range or retain loyal customers). Portfolio logic: cows fund question marks to become stars.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a business rationally KEEP a product classified as a dog?","options":{"A":"Dogs always turn into stars eventually.","B":"Halting all promotion since the product is already established in the market, even though this risks losing the loyal, repeat-purchasing customers a dog product still profitably serves.","C":"It may complete a product range, share fixed costs, or serve loyal customers whose other purchases are profitable — classification alone does not decide strategy.","D":"Dogs generate more cash than cash cows."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-14","stem":"A firm has revenue of £500,000, cost of sales £300,000, and other operating expenses £120,000. Its gross profit margin and operating profit margin are:","options":{"A":"Gross 60%, operating 24% — calculated by dividing each by total costs.","B":"Gross 40% (200k ÷ 500k), operating 16% (80k ÷ 500k)","C":"Gross 16%, operating 40% — operating profit is always higher than gross profit.","D":"Both 40% — gross and operating margins are always identical by accounting definition."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Gross profit = revenue − cost of sales = 200,000; GPM = 40%. Operating profit = gross profit − operating expenses = 80,000; OPM = 16%. The GAP between the two margins reveals overhead weight. Improving GPM: raise prices, cut direct costs, change supplier. Improving OPM: control overheads. Interpretation needs comparison — against last year, competitors, or industry norms; a 'good' margin is context-dependent (supermarkets thin, software fat).","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's gross margin is stable but its operating margin has fallen sharply over two years. The most likely cause is:","options":{"A":"Rising raw material costs.","B":"Falling selling prices.","C":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.","D":"Rising overheads such as rent, salaries or marketing — costs below gross profit but above operating profit."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-15","stem":"An established profitable business needs finance for a major expansion. Compared with a bank loan, funding it from retained profit has which key advantage and drawback?","options":{"A":"How many employees will receive training, a workforce-planning question unrelated to whether retained profit or a loan is the better source of expansion finance.","B":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.","C":"Retained profit: no interest or repayment and no dilution, but it depletes reserves and is limited to what has been accumulated.","D":"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Retained profit: internal, free of interest, no dilution — but finite, may disappoint shareholders expecting dividends, and depletes reserves. Loan: fast, preserves ownership, interest is tax-deductible — but repayments strain cash flow, may need security, raises gearing. Share issue: large sums, no repayment — but dilutes control, dividend expectations. Match source to context: amount needed, urgency, risk appetite, current gearing. Evaluation: 'it depends on' the firm's existing gearing and cash position.","reforge":{"stem":"A highly geared firm (existing debt is large relative to equity) needs expansion finance. Why might further loans be unwise?","options":{"A":"Additional debt raises fixed interest commitments and insolvency risk if revenues dip, and lenders will charge more or refuse — equity or retained profit spreads the risk better.","B":"How long it takes to recover the initial investment, an investment-appraisal measure lenders use instead of gearing when deciding whether further borrowing for expansion is genuinely affordable.","C":"Loans are illegal above 50% gearing.","D":"Banks only lend to new businesses."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-16","stem":"Sales of a mature product have begun to decline. The marketing team proposes 'extension strategies'. Which of the following is an extension strategy, and what is its purpose?","options":{"A":"Deleting the product immediately to free up resources for new development.","B":"Halting all promotion since the product is already established in the market.","C":"Doubling the price to maximise revenue from the remaining loyal customers.","D":"Repositioning for a new segment, updating packaging, or finding new uses"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Product life cycle: development → introduction → growth → maturity → decline. Extension strategies at maturity/early decline: new segments or markets, product modification, new uses, repackaging, price adjustment, promotion refresh. Evaluation: extension spends money on a possibly doomed product — the alternative is investing in new products (link to Boston Matrix portfolio decisions). Cash flow varies by stage: negative in development/introduction, strongest in maturity.","reforge":{"stem":"At which life-cycle stage is a product's CASH FLOW typically most negative, and why?","options":{"A":"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.","B":"Development — costs are incurred (research, design, testing) while the product earns no revenue at all.","C":"Decline — sales are falling.","D":"Growth — sales are rising too fast."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.2"},{"id":"BUS-T2-01","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Contribution per unit is calculated as:","options":{"A":"selling price less variable cost per unit","B":"The business must have been miscounting revenue as profit.","C":"revenue less capital employed","D":"fixed costs divided by output"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Contribution shows how much each unit contributes towards fixed costs and profit.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: Contribution per unit is calculated as:","options":{"A":"selling price less variable cost per unit","B":"Cutting the wage bill by making staff redundant.","C":"revenue less capital employed","D":"fixed costs divided by output"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-02","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"If fixed costs rise while contribution per unit is unchanged, break-even output will:","options":{"A":"decrease to zero","B":"increase","C":"remain unchanged","D":"become equal to total revenue"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even output equals fixed costs divided by contribution per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: If fixed costs rise while contribution per unit is unchanged, break-even output will:","options":{"A":"25% of fixed costs.","B":"increase","C":"remain unchanged","D":"become equal to total revenue"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-T2-03","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A cash-flow forecast is mainly used to predict:","options":{"A":"annual depreciation only","B":"Retained profit: improves gearing to 100% and shareholders must approve the use.","C":"timing of cash inflows and outflows","D":"the firm's brand value"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Cash timing matters because profitable sales may be paid for later.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A cash-flow forecast is mainly used to predict:","options":{"A":"the exact market share of each rival","B":"the market share of competitors","C":"timing of cash inflows and outflows","D":"the firm's brand value"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-T2-04","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A cash-flow deficit means:","options":{"A":"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets.","B":"sales revenue exceeds variable cost","C":"assets exceed liabilities","D":"planned cash outflows exceed inflows in a period"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"A deficit signals a funding need even if the income statement shows profit.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A cash-flow deficit means:","options":{"A":"whether the business will make an accounting profit","B":"sales revenue exceeds variable cost","C":"assets exceed liabilities","D":"planned cash outflows exceed inflows in a period"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-T2-05","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Retained profit is an internal source of finance because it is:","options":{"A":"profit kept in the business rather than distributed","B":"a loan from a commercial bank","C":"Is available to every business regardless of trading history.","D":"a supplier's credit period"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Retained earnings do not require an external lender or new owner.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: Retained profit is an internal source of finance because it is:","options":{"A":"profit kept in the business rather than distributed","B":"How many employees will receive training, a workforce-planning question unrelated to why retained profit counts as an internal source of finance.","C":"money raised by issuing new shares","D":"a supplier's credit period"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-T2-06","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Gross profit equals:","options":{"A":"revenue minus every business cost","B":"revenue minus cost of sales","C":"fixed costs plus variable costs","D":"net profit plus dividends"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Gross profit is calculated before operating expenses and finance costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: Gross profit equals:","options":{"A":"Revenue divided by total costs, times 100.","B":"revenue minus cost of sales","C":"fixed costs plus variable costs","D":"net profit plus dividends"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-07","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A higher gross profit margin may result from:","options":{"A":"higher interest charges","B":"a fall in selling prices with unchanged costs","C":"a lower cost of sales relative to revenue","D":"greater corporation tax"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Margin compares gross profit with revenue, so cost control can improve it.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A higher gross profit margin may result from:","options":{"A":"Net profit divided by the number of shares.","B":"a fall in selling prices with unchanged costs","C":"a lower cost of sales relative to revenue","D":"greater corporation tax"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-08","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Net profit margin measures:","options":{"A":"fixed costs as a percentage of assets","B":"cash held per employee","C":"revenue divided by output","D":"net profit as a percentage of revenue"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Net profit margin indicates how much of each pound of revenue remains after relevant costs.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: Net profit margin measures:","options":{"A":"Revenue exceeds total variable costs.","B":"cash held per employee","C":"revenue divided by output","D":"net profit as a percentage of revenue"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-09","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"An overdraft is most suitable for:","options":{"A":"short-term fluctuations in working capital","B":"funding a factory for thirty years","C":"Interest payments would fall as gearing rises.","D":"replacing all shareholder equity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Overdrafts are flexible but can be expensive and repayable on demand.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: An overdraft is most suitable for:","options":{"A":"short-term fluctuations in working capital","B":"Costs nothing to run once set up.","C":"the present value of all future cash flows only","D":"replacing all shareholder equity"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-T2-10","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Trade credit allows a business to:","options":{"A":"It has recorded revenue it never actually earned.","B":"receive supplies now and pay the supplier later","C":"avoid recording purchases","D":"sell shares without regulation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Trade credit supports cash flow but may be lost if payment is late.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: Trade credit allows a business to:","options":{"A":"Has no effect on the timing of any cash movement.","B":"receive supplies now and pay the supplier later","C":"avoid recording purchases","D":"sell shares without regulation"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-T2-11","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A balance sheet shows a firm's:","options":{"A":"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets.","B":"cash inflows for each week","C":"assets, liabilities and capital at a point in time","D":"marketing objectives only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"It is a snapshot of financial position, not a forecast of cash timing.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A balance sheet shows a firm's:","options":{"A":"whether the business will make an accounting profit","B":"How many employees will receive training, a workforce-planning question unrelated to what a balance sheet actually records.","C":"assets, liabilities and capital at a point in time","D":"marketing objectives only"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-12","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Working capital is calculated as:","options":{"A":"The average annual return as a percentage.","B":"revenue minus fixed costs","C":"profit divided by sales","D":"current assets minus current liabilities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Positive working capital can help meet short-term obligations.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: Working capital is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Fixed costs rise faster than output does.","B":"revenue minus fixed costs","C":"profit divided by sales","D":"current assets minus current liabilities"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-13","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A current ratio below 1 may suggest:","options":{"A":"current liabilities exceed current assets","B":"It has no fixed costs to cover each month.","C":"profit is necessarily negative","D":"sales are growing too quickly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Liquidity may be weak, although the appropriate ratio depends on the industry.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A current ratio below 1 may suggest:","options":{"A":"current liabilities exceed current assets","B":"Holds excessive cash reserves in the bank.","C":"profit is necessarily negative","D":"sales are growing too quickly"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-14","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Investment appraisal using payback focuses on:","options":{"A":"Is available to every business regardless of trading history.","B":"how long it takes to recover the initial investment","C":"the percentage of market share","D":"the firm's gross margin"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Payback is simple and highlights liquidity, but ignores later cash flows and time value.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: Investment appraisal using payback focuses on:","options":{"A":"In more than one country, a description of multinational operations rather than what the payback method in investment appraisal actually measures.","B":"how long it takes to recover the initial investment","C":"the percentage of market share","D":"the firm's gross margin"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-15","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"Net present value is positive when:","options":{"A":"the payback period is longest","B":"accounting profit is zero","C":"discounted expected inflows exceed the initial investment","D":"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"NPV incorporates time value of money and indicates value created at the chosen discount rate.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: Net present value is positive when:","options":{"A":"non-current liabilities only","B":"How many employees will receive training, a workforce-planning question unrelated to when net present value turns positive.","C":"discounted expected inflows exceed the initial investment","D":"Is available to every business regardless of trading history."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-16","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A business with high gearing has relatively:","options":{"A":"Increases cash outflow in the current period.","B":"low fixed costs compared with output","C":"many more employees than competitors","D":"high long-term debt compared with capital"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Gearing indicates reliance on long-term borrowing and the associated financial risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A business with high gearing has relatively:","options":{"A":"Raises total profit for the financial year.","B":"low fixed costs compared with output","C":"many more employees than competitors","D":"high long-term debt compared with capital"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-T2-17","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A fall in the value of a firm's inventory may reduce:","options":{"A":"current assets and working capital","B":"non-current liabilities only","C":"the number of ordinary shares","D":"Loans are illegal above 50% gearing."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Inventory is a current asset and its valuation affects the balance sheet.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A fall in the value of a firm's inventory may reduce:","options":{"A":"current assets and working capital","B":"Fixed costs rise faster than output does.","C":"the number of ordinary shares","D":"the legal identity of the firm"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-FINPLAN-01","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A cash-flow forecast is mainly used to predict: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Retained profit: improves gearing to 100% and shareholders must approve the use.","B":"when cash is expected to enter and leave the business","C":"the exact market share of each rival","D":"how many employees will receive training"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Forecasts help managers anticipate liquidity problems and plan finance.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: A cash-flow forecast is mainly used to predict: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Buying a company with permanent finance, a description of an acquisition rather than what a cash-flow forecast is used to predict.","B":"when cash is expected to enter and leave the business","C":"the exact market share of each rival","D":"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-FINPLAN-02","spec":"BUS-2","stem":"A business reaches its break-even output when:","options":{"A":"variable costs become zero","B":"fixed costs are higher than revenue","C":"total revenue equals total costs","D":"profit is at its maximum"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"At break-even, the contribution made by output covers fixed costs and profit is zero.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A business reaches its break-even output when:","options":{"A":"8,000 units — the break-even level itself.","B":"fixed costs are higher than revenue","C":"total revenue equals total costs","D":"profit is at its maximum"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-N2-01","stem":"Which calculation gives the contribution earned on each unit sold?","options":{"A":"Fixed costs divided by units sold.","B":"Selling price minus total fixed costs.","C":"Total revenue minus total costs.","D":"Selling price less variable cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Contribution per unit = selling price − variable cost per unit. Total contribution = contribution per unit × units sold, and this contributes first to fixed costs, then to profit. Profit = total contribution − fixed costs. Break-even output = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit. Keeping contribution and profit distinct is essential: a product with positive contribution may still leave the firm loss-making if fixed costs are not covered.","reforge":{"stem":"A product sells for £30 with variable costs of £18. Fixed costs are £60,000. Break-even output is:","options":{"A":"2,000 units, from £60,000 ÷ £30.","B":"5,000 units, from £60,000 ÷ £12","C":"3,333 units, from £60,000 ÷ £18.","D":"60,000 units, equal to the fixed costs."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-02","stem":"The margin of safety measures the amount by which:","options":{"A":"Current output exceeds break-even output","B":"Revenue exceeds total variable costs.","C":"Fixed costs exceed contribution earned.","D":"Selling price exceeds the nearest competitor's."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Margin of safety = current (or budgeted) output − break-even output, sometimes shown as a percentage of current output. It indicates how far sales can fall before losses begin, so a larger margin means lower risk. It can be widened by raising price, cutting variable costs, reducing fixed costs or increasing sales volume. Break-even analysis assumes costs and price are constant, which limits its use in volatile markets.","reforge":{"stem":"A key limitation of break-even analysis is its assumption that:","options":{"A":"Fixed costs vary directly with output.","B":"All output produced is given away free.","C":"Selling price stays constant throughout","D":"Contribution per unit is always negative."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-N2-03","stem":"A firm is profitable but runs out of cash. The most likely explanation is that:","options":{"A":"It has no fixed costs to cover each month.","B":"Customers pay long after sales are recorded","C":"Its selling price is below variable cost.","D":"It has recorded revenue it never actually earned."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Profit is revenue less costs over a period; cash flow is money actually entering and leaving. They diverge because credit sales are recorded as revenue before cash arrives, stock is paid for before it sells, capital purchases consume cash without appearing as an expense, and depreciation is a cost with no cash movement. Rapidly growing firms often fail through overtrading: profitable on paper but unable to fund working capital.","reforge":{"stem":"Overtrading occurs when a business:","options":{"A":"Deliberately reduces its sales volume.","B":"Sells only to cash-paying customers.","C":"Holds excessive cash reserves in the bank.","D":"Expands beyond its working capital"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-04","stem":"Improving a cash-flow forecast by extending supplier payment terms:","options":{"A":"Increases cash outflow in the current period.","B":"Raises total profit for the financial year.","C":"Delays outflows, straining suppliers","D":"Has no effect on the timing of any cash movement."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Cash flow can be improved by speeding inflows — chasing debtors, offering settlement discounts, factoring invoices, taking deposits — or delaying outflows through longer supplier credit and leasing rather than buying. Each has a cost: discounts reduce revenue, factoring charges a fee, and stretching suppliers can damage relationships or lose favourable terms. Note these timing measures change cash, not profit.","reforge":{"stem":"Debt factoring improves cash flow but reduces profit because the factor:","options":{"A":"Charges a fee for advancing the money","B":"Refuses to collect from any late payers.","C":"Requires the firm to increase its prices.","D":"Delays payment until customers settle in full."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-N2-05","stem":"Retained profit as a source of finance has the advantage that it:","options":{"A":"Is available to every business regardless of trading history.","B":"Requires no approval from the company's owners.","C":"Provides an unlimited amount of funding.","D":"Carries no interest and no loss of ownership"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Internal finance — retained profit, sale of assets, tighter working capital — is cheap and involves no interest or dilution, but is limited in scale and unavailable to new firms. External finance includes bank loans (interest, often secured), overdrafts (flexible, expensive, repayable on demand), share capital (no repayment but dilutes ownership and control), venture capital, leasing and trade credit. Choice depends on purpose, amount, cost, risk and control.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm needs finance for a machine it will use for ten years. The least appropriate source is:","options":{"A":"A ten-year bank loan secured on the asset.","B":"A bank overdraft repayable on demand","C":"An issue of new ordinary shares.","D":"Retained profit accumulated over past years."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-06","stem":"A firm's gross profit margin is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Gross profit divided by revenue, times 100","B":"Net profit divided by the number of shares.","C":"Operating profit divided by capital employed.","D":"Revenue divided by total costs, times 100."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Gross profit = revenue − cost of sales; gross margin = gross profit ÷ revenue × 100, showing how efficiently the firm buys and produces. Operating profit deducts overheads; net profit deducts interest and tax. A falling gross margin with a stable net margin points to rising input or production costs; a stable gross margin with a falling net margin points to rising overheads, interest or tax. Margins are best judged against trend and industry norms.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's gross margin is unchanged but its net margin has fallen. The most likely cause is:","options":{"A":"An increase in the cost of raw materials.","B":"A fall in the selling price of its products.","C":"A rise in overheads or interest charges","D":"A reduction in the volume of goods sold."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-07","stem":"A current ratio of 0.6:1 suggests that a business:","options":{"A":"Holds too much cash in low-return accounts.","B":"May struggle to meet short-term liabilities","C":"Is highly profitable relative to its competitors.","D":"Has no long-term borrowings outstanding."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities, with roughly 1.5–2:1 often cited as comfortable, though the right level varies by sector. Below 1:1 means short-term debts exceed short-term assets, indicating possible liquidity difficulty. Far above 2:1 may mean cash or stock sitting idle. The acid test ratio excludes stock, since stock may not convert to cash quickly, and is the stricter measure for firms holding slow-moving inventory.","reforge":{"stem":"The acid test ratio differs from the current ratio because it excludes:","options":{"A":"Trade receivables owed by customers.","B":"Cash held in the firm's bank account.","C":"All of the firm's current liabilities.","D":"Inventory, which may be slow to sell"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-08","stem":"A high gearing ratio indicates that a firm is:","options":{"A":"Generating high profits relative to sales.","B":"Holding an unusually large cash balance.","C":"Financed largely by long-term borrowing","D":"Owned entirely by its original founders."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Gearing = non-current liabilities ÷ capital employed × 100; above roughly 50% is usually considered highly geared. High gearing means greater reliance on debt: interest must be paid regardless of trading, so profits and cash are vulnerable to rate rises and downturns, but owners keep control and returns are magnified in good years. Low gearing is safer but may mean the firm is missing growth opportunities.","reforge":{"stem":"A highly geared firm is most exposed to which external change?","options":{"A":"A rise in interest rates on its borrowing","B":"A fall in the corporation tax rate.","C":"An increase in consumer disposable income.","D":"A strengthening of the domestic currency."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-09","stem":"Payback period as an investment appraisal method measures:","options":{"A":"The total profit an investment earns over its life.","B":"The average annual return as a percentage.","C":"The present value of all future cash flows.","D":"The time taken to recover the initial outlay"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Payback = time to recover the initial cost, favouring liquidity and quick returns; simple to calculate but it ignores all cash flows after payback and the time value of money. Average rate of return expresses average annual profit as a percentage of the investment, capturing the whole life but still ignoring timing. Net present value discounts future flows to today's value, which is the most complete but depends on the discount rate chosen.","reforge":{"stem":"Net present value is considered superior to payback because it:","options":{"A":"Requires no estimate of future cash flows.","B":"Accounts for the time value of money","C":"Always produces a positive result.","D":"Can be calculated without a discount rate."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-10","stem":"A budget variance is described as adverse when:","options":{"A":"Actual costs are higher than budgeted","B":"The budget was set at the start of the year.","C":"Actual and budgeted figures are identical.","D":"Actual revenue exceeds the budgeted figure."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"A favourable variance improves profit: revenue above budget or costs below it. An adverse variance worsens profit: revenue below budget or costs above it. Note the direction matters more than the sign — higher costs are adverse, higher revenue is favourable. Variance analysis directs attention to where actual performance diverges from plan, but the cause must then be investigated: an adverse labour variance might reflect wage rises, inefficiency, or an over-optimistic budget.","reforge":{"stem":"Zero-based budgeting differs from historical budgeting because each cost must be:","options":{"A":"Increased in line with the rate of inflation.","B":"Set at exactly the previous year's level.","C":"Justified afresh, not from last year","D":"Approved by the firm's external auditors."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-11","stem":"Delegated budgets are most likely to improve motivation because managers:","options":{"A":"Are no longer accountable for their spending.","B":"Have authority over spending in their own area","C":"Can ignore the firm's overall corporate objectives.","D":"Face no consequences for adverse variances."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Delegating budgets gives junior managers authority over their own area, which can motivate through responsibility and produce better-informed decisions from people closest to the work. Risks: managers may pursue departmental rather than corporate goals, budgets may be inflated to create slack, and weak financial skills can lead to poor control. Effective delegation therefore needs training, clear limits and review against corporate objectives.","reforge":{"stem":"A risk of delegating budgets is that departmental managers may:","options":{"A":"Lose all interest in controlling their costs.","B":"Be unable to spend any of the allocation.","C":"Automatically reduce their own headcount.","D":"Build in slack by overstating their needs"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.4"},{"id":"BUS-N2-12","stem":"A firm's promotional mix refers to the combination of:","options":{"A":"Product, price, place and promotion decisions.","B":"Fixed and variable costs in the marketing budget.","C":"Methods used to communicate with customers","D":"Distribution channels reaching the final consumer."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRICING","scaffold":"The promotional mix covers advertising, sales promotion, personal selling, public relations, direct marketing and digital channels. The choice depends on the target audience, budget, product type and stage of the life cycle: personal selling suits complex high-value industrial goods, mass advertising suits fast-moving consumer goods. Do not confuse the promotional mix with the marketing mix, which is the wider 4Ps or 7Ps.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm selling complex machinery to a small number of industrial buyers should emphasise:","options":{"A":"Personal selling by a technical sales team","B":"National television advertising campaigns.","C":"Money-off coupons in consumer magazines.","D":"Social media influencer partnerships."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-13","stem":"Distribution through a wholesaler rather than direct to retailers usually means the producer:","options":{"A":"Retains complete control of the final selling price.","B":"Deals directly with every end consumer.","C":"Earns a higher margin on each unit sold.","D":"Reaches more small retailers at lower cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Longer channels — producer to wholesaler to retailer to consumer — reach many small outlets efficiently, with the wholesaler breaking bulk and holding stock, but each intermediary takes a margin and the producer loses control over presentation and price. Direct channels, including e-commerce, protect margin and customer relationship but require the producer to handle storage, fulfilment and marketing itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A producer switching to selling direct online gains mainly:","options":{"A":"Reduced responsibility for order fulfilment.","B":"A higher margin and direct customer data","C":"Lower spending on marketing activity.","D":"Guaranteed shelf space in major retailers."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-14","stem":"A strong brand allows a business to:","options":{"A":"Charge a premium and build repeat custom","B":"Eliminate the need for any market research.","C":"Avoid competition law and consumer regulation.","D":"Guarantee its costs will never increase."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Brands create recognition, trust and emotional association, supporting premium pricing, repeat purchase, easier launch of new products and stronger bargaining power with retailers. Building one requires sustained investment in quality, consistency and promotion, and the value can be destroyed quickly by a quality or ethical failure. Brand strength typically makes demand less price elastic, since substitutes feel less acceptable to loyal buyers.","reforge":{"stem":"Successful branding usually makes demand for a product:","options":{"A":"Perfectly elastic at the market price.","B":"More price elastic, since buyers compare more.","C":"Less price elastic, since buyers are loyal","D":"Completely unrelated to the price charged."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-N2-15","stem":"Digital marketing offers a measurable advantage over traditional advertising because it:","options":{"A":"Removes the need to define a target market.","B":"Tracks response and targets behaviour","C":"Guarantees a higher conversion rate.","D":"Costs nothing to run once set up."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-MARKET-RESEARCH","scaffold":"Digital channels allow precise targeting by demographics, interests and past behaviour, real-time measurement of clicks and conversions, rapid adjustment of campaigns, and lower entry cost than broadcast media. Limits: ad fatigue and blockers, privacy regulation restricting tracking, reliance on platform algorithms the firm does not control, and reputational risk from public complaints spreading quickly.","reforge":{"stem":"A constraint on behavioural targeting in digital marketing is:","options":{"A":"The impossibility of measuring click rates.","B":"The inability to change a campaign once live.","C":"The high cost relative to television advertising.","D":"Data protection law limiting use of personal data"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-16","stem":"A firm holding buffer stock is primarily protecting itself against:","options":{"A":"A fall in the market value of its shares.","B":"An increase in its long-term interest costs.","C":"Unexpected demand or delivery delays","D":"Changes in corporation tax legislation."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Buffer stock is the minimum level held to absorb unexpected demand or late supplier delivery, preventing lost sales and production stoppages. It ties up cash, occupies storage and risks obsolescence. Just-in-time minimises stock, cutting holding costs and freeing capital, but demands reliable suppliers and leaves no cushion against disruption. The choice trades holding cost against the cost and likelihood of running out.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm adopting just-in-time stock control becomes more vulnerable to:","options":{"A":"Disruption in its supply chain","B":"Stock becoming obsolete before sale.","C":"Cash being tied up in unsold inventory.","D":"Rising warehouse and storage costs."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-17","stem":"Capacity utilisation is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Maximum output divided by current output.","B":"Units produced divided by employees.","C":"Total revenue divided by total fixed costs.","D":"Current output over maximum output"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Capacity utilisation = current output ÷ maximum possible output × 100. High utilisation spreads fixed costs over more units, lowering unit cost, but leaves no slack for extra orders, machine maintenance or staff pressure. Low utilisation means idle capacity and high unit fixed costs. Around 90% is often seen as a working optimum. Capacity can be flexed through subcontracting, temporary staff, overtime or rationalisation.","reforge":{"stem":"Operating at 98% capacity for a sustained period risks:","options":{"A":"Fixed costs per unit rising sharply.","B":"No slack for maintenance or extra orders","C":"An inability to sell the output produced.","D":"A permanent fall in labour productivity."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-N2-18","stem":"Economies of scale cause a firm's:","options":{"A":"Average cost per unit to fall","B":"Fixed costs to become variable costs.","C":"Selling price to rise with higher volume.","D":"Total costs to fall as output increases."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Internal economies include purchasing (bulk discounts), technical (larger, more efficient plant), managerial (specialist staff), marketing (fixed campaign cost spread wider) and financial (cheaper borrowing). External economies arise from the industry's growth in an area. Diseconomies follow from excessive size: communication breakdown, coordination difficulty, weaker motivation. Note total cost still rises with output; it is average cost that falls.","reforge":{"stem":"Diseconomies of scale most commonly arise from:","options":{"A":"Bulk-buying discounts from major suppliers.","B":"Spreading marketing costs over more units.","C":"Poor communication as the firm grows","D":"Access to cheaper long-term borrowing."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-N2-19","stem":"Lean production aims principally to:","options":{"A":"Maximise stock levels held at every stage.","B":"Reduce waste across the production process","C":"Increase the number of quality inspectors.","D":"Lengthen the product development cycle."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Lean production minimises waste of time, materials, movement, defects and stock. Techniques include just-in-time, kaizen (continuous small improvements suggested by staff), cell production, and total quality management, which builds quality into the process rather than inspecting it at the end. Benefits are lower cost, better quality and faster response; risks are vulnerability to disruption and the cultural change required of the workforce.","reforge":{"stem":"Kaizen differs from a one-off efficiency programme because it relies on:","options":{"A":"Inspecting finished goods more thoroughly.","B":"A single large investment in new machinery.","C":"External consultants redesigning the factory.","D":"Continuous small staff improvements"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-N2-20","stem":"Total quality management differs from traditional quality control because it:","options":{"A":"Inspects only a random sample of finished goods.","B":"Applies solely to the firm's suppliers.","C":"Makes every employee responsible for quality","D":"Relies on a separate quality department."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Quality control inspects output at the end and rejects or reworks defects, so waste has already occurred. Quality assurance builds checks into each stage. Total quality management goes further, making quality every employee's responsibility with a culture of getting it right first time and treating the next stage as an internal customer. TQM reduces waste and rework but requires training, commitment and time before benefits appear.","reforge":{"stem":"A benefit of preventing defects rather than inspecting for them is that the firm:","options":{"A":"Avoids the cost of rework and scrapped output","B":"Needs no staff training in quality methods.","C":"Can raise its selling price automatically.","D":"No longer needs to monitor customer feedback."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-N2-21","stem":"A firm's unit cost will fall if:","options":{"A":"Output falls while fixed costs are unchanged.","B":"Fixed costs rise faster than output does.","C":"Variable cost per unit increases sharply.","D":"Output rises while fixed costs stay the same"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Unit cost = total cost ÷ output. Because fixed costs are spread over more units, raising output lowers unit cost even when variable cost per unit is constant — the basis of economies of scale. Unit cost also falls if variable cost per unit is reduced through cheaper inputs, better productivity or less waste. Lower unit cost supports either wider margins or more competitive pricing.","reforge":{"stem":"Fixed costs are £40,000. At 4,000 units the fixed cost per unit is £10. At 8,000 units it becomes:","options":{"A":"£20, since costs double with output.","B":"£5, spread over more units","C":"£10, since fixed costs never change.","D":"£40,000, unchanged in total per unit."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"},{"id":"BUS-N2-22","stem":"A firm's working capital is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Current assets less liabilities","B":"Non-current assets minus depreciation.","C":"Share capital plus retained profit.","D":"Total assets minus total liabilities."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Working capital = current assets − current liabilities, funding day-to-day operations: paying suppliers and wages while waiting for customers to pay. Too little risks being unable to meet obligations even while profitable; too much means cash, stock or receivables sitting idle instead of earning a return. It is managed by controlling inventory, chasing receivables promptly and negotiating supplier terms.","reforge":{"stem":"Reducing the time customers take to pay would most directly improve a firm's:","options":{"A":"Gross profit margin on each sale.","B":"Gearing ratio and long-term debt.","C":"Working capital and cash position","D":"Capacity utilisation in the factory."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.3"},{"id":"BUS-N5-02","stem":"Depreciation appears in a firm's accounts as:","options":{"A":"A cash payment made to the asset's supplier.","B":"A cost with no matching cash outflow","C":"An increase in the firm's current assets.","D":"A form of dividend paid to shareholders."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-CASH-FINANCE","scaffold":"Depreciation spreads the cost of a non-current asset across its useful life, matching the cost to the periods that benefit. It reduces recorded profit and the asset's book value, but no cash leaves the business in that period — the cash went when the asset was bought. This is a common source of confusion between profit and cash flow, and it is why depreciation is added back when converting profit into operating cash flow.","reforge":{"stem":"When converting operating profit into cash flow, depreciation is added back because it:","options":{"A":"Is a payment made to the tax authority.","B":"Represents cash received from customers.","C":"Increases the value of the asset held.","D":"Never involved an outflow of cash"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.1"}]},"BUS-3":{"label":"Operations, Strategy & External Influences","color":"#78350f","questions":[{"id":"OPS-01","stem":"Labour productivity is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Output per worker per time period (total output ÷ number of workers)","B":"Total revenue ÷ number of employees","C":"Profit per employee per year","D":"Quality control is more effective because it catches every defective product."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Labour productivity = output per worker per time period. Formula: total output ÷ number of workers. High productivity = more output from the same number of workers = lower unit labour cost. Ways to improve: training, better equipment/technology, improved motivation, lean production methods. Exam trap: confusing productivity with profitability or efficiency — productivity is specifically about output per worker, not about costs or revenues directly.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory employs 50 workers and produces 10,000 units per week. Labour productivity is:","options":{"A":"500,000 units","B":"Total revenue divided by the number of employees.","C":"200 units per worker per week — 10,000 ÷ 50","D":"10,000 units per week"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"OPS-02","stem":"Lean production aims to eliminate waste. Which of the following is NOT one of the seven wastes identified in lean thinking?","options":{"A":"Overproduction — making more than is currently needed","B":"Skilled labour — having too many qualified employees","C":"Waiting — idle time when goods are not being processed","D":"Transportation — unnecessary movement of materials"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"The seven wastes (TIMWOOD): Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Overprocessing, Defects. Skilled labour is not a waste — lean production values worker expertise and multi-skilling. The philosophy: eliminate activities that consume resources without adding value from the customer's perspective. Just-in-time (JIT) directly addresses overproduction and inventory waste. Kaizen (continuous improvement) targets all seven through small, incremental changes.","reforge":{"stem":"A car manufacturer holds 6 weeks of component stock 'just in case'. Which lean waste does this represent, and what is the lean alternative?","options":{"A":"Overproduction — the solution is to make more cars.","B":"The cost savings a business achieves from operating at a larger scale, leading to a fall in average unit cost as output rises — a definition of economies of scale, not a lean waste category at all.","C":"Motion waste — workers are moving too much.","D":"Inventory waste — holding excess stock ties up capital and hides quality problems. The lean alternative is JIT: receive components only when needed for production, reducing inventory to near-zero."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"OPS-03","stem":"A rise in interest rates is most likely to affect a business by:","options":{"A":"Reducing the business's tax liability","B":"Increasing consumer disposable income","C":"Raising borrowing costs, dampening demand","D":"Reducing the cost of imported raw materials"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Interest rate effects on business: (1) Borrowing costs rise — loan repayments increase, reducing profitability; firms with high gearing are most exposed; (2) Consumer spending falls — mortgage costs rise, reducing disposable income; demand for big-ticket items (cars, kitchens) falls most; (3) Exchange rate: higher rates attract foreign investment → currency appreciates → exports more expensive, imports cheaper. Evaluation: impact depends on gearing, whether products are necessities or luxuries, and export dependency.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are highly geared businesses more vulnerable to interest rate rises than low-geared businesses?","options":{"A":"Highly geared firms face larger repayment increases","B":"Highly geared firms have more employees to pay, increasing fixed labour costs.","C":"Gearing affects employee productivity levels rather than the firm's financial costs.","D":"Highly geared firms always operate with permanently lower profit margins."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.5"},{"id":"OPS-04","stem":"Porter's Five Forces model analyses industry attractiveness. Which force does the threat of substitute products represent?","options":{"A":"The threat of new entrants into the market","B":"The bargaining power of suppliers","C":"The intensity of competitive rivalry","D":"Buyers switching to substitutes outside it"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Porter's Five Forces: (1) Competitive rivalry; (2) Threat of new entrants; (3) Bargaining power of suppliers; (4) Bargaining power of buyers; (5) Threat of substitutes. Substitutes come from OUTSIDE the industry: Uber is a substitute for taxis; streaming is a substitute for cinema; cycling is a substitute for public transport. High substitute threat caps pricing power and profit margins. Exam trap: confusing substitutes (from outside) with competitors (within the same industry).","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following represents a substitute threat to the cinema industry?","options":{"A":"A new cinema chain opening nearby and offering lower ticket prices.","B":"Streaming films at home substitutes for cinema","C":"Popcorn suppliers raising their wholesale prices to cinemas.","D":"Odeon acquiring Vue cinemas, reducing competition in the local market."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"OPS-05","stem":"A business conducts a SWOT analysis and identifies 'increasing consumer demand for sustainable products' as an element. This is best classified as:","options":{"A":"An opportunity — an external trend the business could exploit to gain competitive advantage.","B":"Hygiene factors motivate workers and motivators prevent dissatisfaction (this is Herzberg reversed).","C":"A threat — competitors may exploit this trend first.","D":"A strength — the business values sustainability."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"SWOT: Strengths and Weaknesses = internal (the business itself); Opportunities and Threats = external (the environment). A market trend — regardless of whether it is positive or negative — is external. Rising demand for sustainability is an external development the business can exploit (opportunity) or may be unable to respond to (threat if competitors move faster). The classification depends on the firm's position: a firm already investing in sustainability sees this as an opportunity; a firm reliant on non-sustainable practices may classify it as a threat.","reforge":{"stem":"A business is highly dependent on one supplier who has just announced a 30% price increase. In a SWOT analysis, this is:","options":{"A":"Netflix releasing cinema films on streaming — consumers substitute the same experience at lower cost from home, reducing cinema demand, an example of a threat drawn from a completely different industry.","B":"A strength — the close supplier relationship shows reliability.","C":"A threat — an external development that will increase costs and reduce competitiveness. The firm's dependence on a single supplier is the underlying weakness that makes this threat especially serious.","D":"A weakness — the firm should have negotiated better prices."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"OPS-06","stem":"Quality assurance differs from quality control primarily in that:","options":{"A":"Quality assurance is cheaper because it requires fewer staff.","B":"Quality control checks products after production to identify defects; quality assurance embeds quality throughout the production process to prevent defects arising in the first place.","C":"Quality assurance only applies to service businesses.","D":"The cost savings a business achieves from operating at a larger scale, leading to a fall in average unit cost — a definition of economies of scale, not what distinguishes quality assurance from quality control."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Quality control (QC): inspection at the end of production — catches defects but does not prevent them; faulty products may have been costly to make; relies on inspectors. Quality assurance (QA): prevention-focused — quality standards built into every stage of production; workers take responsibility; aims to get it right first time. Total Quality Management (TQM) extends QA to a whole-company philosophy: zero defects, continuous improvement (kaizen), customer focus. QA is generally preferred — preventing defects is cheaper than finding and scrapping them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is quality assurance generally more cost-effective than quality control in the long run?","options":{"A":"QA requires less staff training than QC inspection.","B":"The total reduction in fixed costs when a firm closes a factory, a rationalisation outcome rather than why quality assurance is more cost-effective than quality control.","C":"QA inspectors receive lower wages than QC inspectors at every level.","D":"QA prevents defects throughout the process, avoiding the cost of scrapping faulty goods; QC only catches defects after production."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"OPS-07","stem":"Economies of scale refer to:","options":{"A":"The increase in average costs as a firm grows beyond its optimal size.","B":"The total reduction in fixed costs when a firm closes a factory, a rationalisation outcome rather than what economies of scale actually describes.","C":"The cost savings a business achieves from operating at a larger scale, leading to a fall in average (unit) cost as output increases.","D":"The benefit of producing in a single location rather than multiple sites."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Economies of scale (EoS): as output rises, average cost per unit falls. Types: purchasing (bulk-buying discount), technical (larger, more efficient machinery), financial (better loan terms for larger firms), managerial (specialists instead of generalists), risk-bearing (diversified product range). Diseconomies of scale: average costs rise beyond optimal size — communication breakdown, coordination problems, motivation falls. Minimum efficient scale: output level where average cost is minimised.","reforge":{"stem":"A supermarket chain negotiates a lower price per unit from a supplier because it orders 10,000 units instead of 1,000. This is an example of which type of economy of scale?","options":{"A":"Purchasing (buying) economies of scale — bulk-buying power gives the larger firm lower unit input costs.","B":"QA inspectors receive lower wages than QC inspectors at every level, a pay claim rather than the type of economy of scale bulk-buying actually represents.","C":"Technical economies — larger machinery is more efficient.","D":"Financial economies — the bank offers better interest rates."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"OPS-08","stem":"The impact of exchange rate depreciation on a UK exporter is best described as:","options":{"A":"Exports become more expensive for foreign buyers, reducing demand.","B":"The UK exporter must raise prices to maintain profit margins.","C":"Exchange rate movements have no effect on exporters who invoice in sterling.","D":"Exports become cheaper abroad, though imports cost more"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Sterling depreciation (£ falls vs foreign currencies): (1) UK exports cheaper in foreign currency → more competitive → higher volume potentially; (2) UK imports more expensive in sterling → higher raw material costs. Net effect on an exporter: revenue opportunity (more sales) vs cost pressure (pricier imports). Evaluation: depends on price elasticity of demand for exports, proportion of imported inputs, and whether contracts are in sterling or foreign currency. Firms invoicing in sterling ARE affected because their prices look cheaper to foreign buyers.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK car manufacturer exports to the EU. The pound depreciates by 15% against the euro. Which best describes the likely short-term effect?","options":{"A":"UK cars become more expensive for EU buyers — exports fall.","B":"UK cars become cheaper for EU buyers in euro terms, potentially increasing demand. However, if the manufacturer uses imported components, input costs rise, squeezing margins. The net effect depends on PED and import dependency.","C":"The manufacturer must reduce sterling prices to maintain market share, because this option treats depreciation as though it always squeezes overseas demand regardless of direction, forcing exporters to cut list prices to stay competitive against EU rivals.","D":"No effect — car prices are set annually in advance."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T3-01","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Labour productivity is output divided by:","options":{"A":"labour input","B":"total revenue","C":"fixed capital only","D":"market demand"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Productivity measures output per unit of labour input.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: Labour productivity is output divided by:","options":{"A":"Profit per employee per year","B":"labour input","C":"fixed capital only","D":"market demand"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-T3-02","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A benefit of just-in-time stock control is:","options":{"A":"complete protection from supply disruption","B":"less capital tied up in inventory","C":"no need for quality checks","D":"guaranteed lower transport costs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"JIT can reduce storage costs but increases dependence on reliable deliveries.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A benefit of just-in-time stock control is:","options":{"A":"Long-term incentive schemes for directors.","B":"no need for quality checks","C":"less capital tied up in inventory","D":"guaranteed lower transport costs"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-T3-03","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Capacity utilisation is actual output as a percentage of:","options":{"A":"Total wage cost divided by units produced.","B":"market demand","C":"maximum possible output","D":"labour turnover"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"High utilisation can lower average fixed cost, though excessive use may reduce flexibility.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: Capacity utilisation is actual output as a percentage of:","options":{"A":"Variable cost per unit is reduced.","B":"market demand","C":"labour turnover","D":"maximum possible output"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.4"},{"id":"BUS-T3-04","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A diseconomy of scale occurs when:","options":{"A":"Transportation waste — components are moved too frequently.","B":"fixed costs become zero","C":"a firm enters a new market","D":"average cost rises as output increases beyond the optimum"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Growth can create communication, coordination and control problems.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A diseconomy of scale occurs when:","options":{"A":"average cost rises as output increases beyond the optimum","B":"Quality assurance is cheaper because it requires fewer staff.","C":"fixed costs become zero","D":"a firm enters a new market"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-T3-05","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Backward vertical integration means acquiring:","options":{"A":"a supplier","B":"a retailer","C":"a direct competitor","D":"an unrelated business"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"The firm moves back towards an earlier stage of its supply chain.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: Backward vertical integration means acquiring:","options":{"A":"A direct competitor at the same stage.","B":"a supplier","C":"a direct competitor","D":"an unrelated business"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-T3-06","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Diversification in the Ansoff Matrix combines:","options":{"A":"an existing product with an existing market","B":"a new product with a new market","C":"a new product with an existing market","D":"an existing product with a new market"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Diversification is the highest-risk Ansoff option because both dimensions are new.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: Diversification in the Ansoff Matrix combines:","options":{"A":"Reducing the business's tax liability","B":"a new product with an existing market","C":"a new product with a new market","D":"an existing product with a new market"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-T3-07","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A cash cow in the Boston Matrix usually has:","options":{"A":"low market share in a high-growth market","B":"low share and low growth","C":"high market share in a low-growth market","D":"high growth but no sales"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Cash cows can generate funds for other products.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A cash cow in the Boston Matrix usually has:","options":{"A":"The cheapest supplier for each material.","B":"low share and low growth","C":"high growth but no sales","D":"high market share in a low-growth market"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-T3-08","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A product extension strategy aims to:","options":{"A":"remove a product immediately","B":"Problem child, needing heavy investment to grow.","C":"prevent any product innovation","D":"extend sales during a mature or declining stage"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Examples include new packaging, new uses, promotion or price changes.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A product extension strategy aims to:","options":{"A":"extend sales during a mature or declining stage","B":"Firms from making any profit at all.","C":"Star, requiring investment to defend its position.","D":"prevent any product innovation"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.3"},{"id":"BUS-T3-09","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A first-mover advantage may result from:","options":{"A":"building recognition before rivals enter","B":"Market development — new market targeted.","C":"waiting until demand disappears","D":"avoiding all development costs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Early entry can create brand loyalty, though it also carries uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A first-mover advantage may result from:","options":{"A":"an invoice payment only","B":"building recognition before rivals enter","C":"Diversification — new product and new market.","D":"avoiding all development costs"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-T3-10","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A merger combines:","options":{"A":"They always have the highest profit margins.","B":"two businesses into one larger organisation","C":"a product with a market segment","D":"a loan with retained profit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Mergers can create synergies but may also face integration problems.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A merger combines:","options":{"A":"They guarantee future growth in market share.","B":"a product with a market segment","C":"two businesses into one larger organisation","D":"a loan with retained profit"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-T3-11","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Organic growth is growth achieved by:","options":{"A":"buying a competitor","B":"Doubling the price to maximise revenue from the remaining loyal customers.","C":"expanding the existing business using its own operations","D":"selling a subsidiary"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Organic growth is usually slower but can be easier to control.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: Organic growth is growth achieved by:","options":{"A":"merges with a supplier","B":"Deleting the product immediately to free up resources for new development.","C":"selling a subsidiary","D":"expanding the existing business using its own operations"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-T3-12","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A decision tree uses probabilities to calculate:","options":{"A":"the exact future outcome","B":"the legal ownership of a firm","C":"No effect — Herzberg said nothing about pay.","D":"expected values for alternative decisions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Expected value combines possible outcomes with their probabilities.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: A decision tree uses probabilities to calculate:","options":{"A":"expected values for alternative decisions","B":"a firm's profit after tax only","C":"the legal ownership of a firm","D":"Motivation will fall because staff become complacent."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-T3-13","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A limitation of expected value is that it:","options":{"A":"assumes outcomes can be represented by probabilities and ignores risk attitudes","B":"guarantees the highest profit","C":"requires no estimates","D":"Motivation will increase significantly — better pay and facilities make staff work harder."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Managers may reject the highest expected value if the risk is unacceptable.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A limitation of expected value is that it:","options":{"A":"Hygiene factors motivate workers and motivators prevent dissatisfaction (this is Herzberg reversed).","B":"assumes outcomes can be represented by probabilities and ignores risk attitudes","C":"requires no estimates","D":"only applies to non-business decisions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-T3-14","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A pressure group differs from a political party because it primarily seeks to:","options":{"A":"maximise shareholder dividends","B":"influence policy on a specific issue rather than form a government","C":"sell products internationally","D":"A new cinema chain opening nearby and offering lower ticket prices."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Pressure groups may lobby, campaign or use publicity without contesting government as a whole.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A pressure group differs from a political party because it primarily seeks to:","options":{"A":"Odeon acquiring Vue cinemas, reducing competition in the local market.","B":"sell products internationally","C":"influence policy on a specific issue rather than form a government","D":"control a firm's production line"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-T3-15","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A contingency plan is designed to:","options":{"A":"Satisfy self-actualisation needs at the top of the hierarchy.","B":"replace every business objective","C":"prepare responses to a possible future disruption","D":"calculate gross profit"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Contingency planning improves resilience when risks materialise.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A contingency plan is designed to:","options":{"A":"Create job enrichment through greater responsibility.","B":"replace every business objective","C":"calculate gross profit","D":"prepare responses to a possible future disruption"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.6"},{"id":"BUS-T3-16","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A business objective should be SMART, meaning it is:","options":{"A":"simple, modern, annual, rapid and technical","B":"social, monetary, active, risky and temporary","C":"Popcorn suppliers raising their wholesale prices to cinemas.","D":"specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and time-bound"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"SMART objectives make progress easier to monitor and evaluate.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A business objective should be SMART, meaning it is:","options":{"A":"specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and time-bound","B":"Demand to remain completely unchanged.","C":"social, monetary, active, risky and temporary","D":"An opportunity — higher costs motivate efficiency improvements."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-T3-17","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A strategic decision is usually:","options":{"A":"long term and concerned with the overall direction of the business","B":"Hygiene factors and motivators are the same concept in different words.","C":"a single customer complaint","D":"an invoice payment only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Strategy allocates resources and sets a broad direction under uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A strategic decision is usually:","options":{"A":"A new cinema chain opening nearby and offering lower ticket prices.","B":"long term and concerned with the overall direction of the business","C":"a single customer complaint","D":"an invoice payment only"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-STRATEGY-01","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"Organic growth occurs when a business:","options":{"A":"Closing the production line and exiting the market.","B":"expands using its own resources and operations","C":"merges with a supplier","D":"closes its original outlets"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"Organic growth develops the existing business rather than combining with another firm.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: Organic growth occurs when a business:","options":{"A":"buying a competitor","B":"merges with a supplier","C":"expands using its own resources and operations","D":"Developing an entirely different product category."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-STRATEGY-02","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A decision tree is useful because it can show:","options":{"A":"A weakness — sustainability is expensive to implement.","B":"the exact behaviour of every competitor","C":"possible outcomes, probabilities and expected values","D":"a firm's profit after tax only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Decision trees structure choices and outcomes where probabilities and returns can be estimated.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A decision tree is useful because it can show:","options":{"A":"the exact future outcome","B":"A strength — the close supplier relationship shows reliability.","C":"a firm's profit after tax only","D":"possible outcomes, probabilities and expected values"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.3"},{"id":"BUS-STRATEGY-03","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A firm can assess its competitiveness by examining:","options":{"A":"only the age of its buildings","B":"the colour of its packaging","C":"A threat — competitors may exploit this trend first.","D":"its costs, quality, market share and customer value"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Competitiveness depends on how well the firm creates value relative to rivals.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A firm can assess its competitiveness by examining:","options":{"A":"its costs, quality, market share and customer value","B":"A weakness — the firm should have negotiated better prices.","C":"the colour of its packaging","D":"whether every employee works remotely"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-STRATEGY-04","spec":"BUS-3","stem":"A possible disadvantage of rapid business growth is:","options":{"A":"communication problems and loss of control","B":"A very narrow span of control at every level.","C":"the removal of all financial risk","D":"an automatic fall in every cost"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Rapid expansion can create diseconomies, coordination problems and pressure on finance.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A possible disadvantage of rapid business growth is:","options":{"A":"closes its original outlets","B":"communication problems and loss of control","C":"More layers of management between top and bottom.","D":"an automatic fall in every cost"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-N3-01","stem":"A rise in interest rates is most likely to reduce demand for a firm selling:","options":{"A":"Basic groceries bought weekly by households.","B":"Products typically bought on credit","C":"Goods sold only for immediate cash payment.","D":"Services with no capital cost to the buyer."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Higher interest rates raise borrowing costs, so demand falls most for big-ticket items usually bought on credit — cars, furniture, housing. Households with mortgages also have less disposable income, and firms face higher costs on existing variable-rate debt and may postpone investment. Savers gain. Highly geared firms and those selling income-elastic goods are hit hardest; sellers of cheap necessities are relatively insulated.","reforge":{"stem":"Besides reducing consumer demand, a rise in interest rates directly affects a highly geared firm by:","options":{"A":"Reducing the corporation tax it must pay.","B":"Lowering the wages it pays employees.","C":"Raising the market value of its shares.","D":"Increasing its interest costs"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.5"},{"id":"BUS-N3-02","stem":"A UK exporter benefits most when the pound:","options":{"A":"Is fixed permanently against the euro.","B":"Strengthens against all other currencies.","C":"Weakens, making its goods cheaper abroad","D":"Rises sharply against the US dollar."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Use SPICED: Strong Pound, Imports Cheaper, Exports Dearer. A weaker pound makes UK exports cheaper in foreign currency, boosting competitiveness and export volumes, but makes imported raw materials and components dearer, squeezing margins for firms reliant on them. A stronger pound reverses both. The net effect depends on how much a firm exports versus imports, and on the price elasticity of its products.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK manufacturer imports most of its components and sells only within the UK. A weaker pound will:","options":{"A":"Raise its input costs, squeezing margins","B":"Reduce the price of its imported components.","C":"Increase its export revenue substantially.","D":"Leave its costs entirely unaffected."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-03","stem":"During a recession, a firm selling luxury goods should expect:","options":{"A":"Demand to rise as consumers trade up.","B":"Its costs to fall in proportion to demand.","C":"Demand to remain completely unchanged.","D":"Demand to fall as incomes are squeezed"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"A recession means falling real GDP, rising unemployment and lower confidence, so income-elastic goods suffer most while inferior goods and cheap necessities may gain. Firms respond by cutting costs, delaying investment, promoting value ranges or targeting more resilient segments. The business cycle — boom, downturn, recession, recovery — shapes which strategies fit, and firms with low gearing and strong cash reserves cope better.","reforge":{"stem":"Which response to a recession is most consistent with maintaining long-term competitiveness?","options":{"A":"Cutting all research and development spending.","B":"Targeting more resilient market segments","C":"Reducing quality to lower the selling price.","D":"Ceasing all staff training immediately."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-04","stem":"An increase in the National Living Wage most directly raises a firm's:","options":{"A":"Labour costs in low-wage sectors","B":"Corporation tax liability for the year.","C":"Spending on imported raw materials.","D":"Interest payments on outstanding loans."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"A higher minimum wage raises costs most in labour-intensive, low-wage sectors such as retail, hospitality and care. Firms may respond by raising prices, accepting lower margins, investing in automation, reducing hours or improving productivity. There can be offsetting benefits: lower turnover, better motivation and higher spending power among low-paid consumers. The net effect depends on labour intensity and the ability to pass costs on.","reforge":{"stem":"A labour-intensive firm facing a large minimum wage rise is most likely to consider:","options":{"A":"Reducing the prices charged to customers.","B":"Increasing the number of low-paid staff.","C":"Investing in automation","D":"Extending credit terms to its suppliers."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-05","stem":"Competition law in the UK primarily aims to prevent:","options":{"A":"Firms from making any profit at all.","B":"Anti-competitive practices","C":"All mergers between competing businesses.","D":"Businesses from advertising to consumers."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Competition authorities act against cartels and price fixing, abuse of a dominant market position, predatory pricing and mergers that would substantially reduce competition. The purpose is to protect consumers through choice, fair prices and innovation. Firms face fines, forced divestment and reputational damage. Not all mergers are blocked — only those likely to harm competition in the relevant market.","reforge":{"stem":"Two rival firms secretly agree to keep prices high. This is:","options":{"A":"Legitimate competitive benchmarking.","B":"A form of penetration pricing.","C":"An example of price discrimination.","D":"A cartel, which is unlawful"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-06","stem":"Consumer protection legislation requires that goods sold are:","options":{"A":"Sold at the lowest price in the market.","B":"Manufactured entirely within the UK.","C":"Of satisfactory quality and as described","D":"Available with unlimited free replacements."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Consumer law requires goods to be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose and as described, with remedies of repair, replacement or refund. Related duties cover accurate advertising, clear pricing and cancellation rights on distance selling. Compliance raises costs through quality systems and returns handling, but protects reputation; breaches bring fines, compensation and lasting damage to trust, which is usually the larger cost.","reforge":{"stem":"The main business case for exceeding minimum legal standards on product safety is:","options":{"A":"Protecting reputation, avoiding recalls","B":"Regulators will exempt the firm from inspection.","C":"It removes the need to hold any insurance.","D":"Competitors are legally required to follow suit."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-07","stem":"A firm pursuing a cost leadership strategy competes primarily by:","options":{"A":"Charging the highest price in the market.","B":"Offering the widest possible product range.","C":"Serving a very small specialist niche only.","D":"Achieving the lowest unit costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Porter's generic strategies: cost leadership — lowest cost base, allowing competitive prices or wider margins, usually needing scale and tight efficiency. Differentiation — a distinctive offer justifying a premium, through brand, quality, design or service. Focus — either cost or differentiation applied to a narrow segment. Porter argued that firms attempting several at once risk being 'stuck in the middle', with no clear advantage.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm with mid-range prices, average quality and no distinctive appeal is described by Porter as:","options":{"A":"Pursuing a successful focus strategy.","B":"Stuck in the middle","C":"An established cost leader.","D":"A clear differentiator in its market."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.5"},{"id":"BUS-N3-08","stem":"In Porter's five forces, the bargaining power of suppliers is greatest when:","options":{"A":"Few suppliers exist and switching is costly","B":"Many suppliers compete for the firm's business.","C":"The input is a standard, widely available commodity.","D":"The buying firm can easily produce the input itself."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Porter's five forces assess industry attractiveness: supplier power, buyer power, threat of new entrants, threat of substitutes, and competitive rivalry. Supplier power rises where suppliers are few, inputs are differentiated or switching costs are high. Buyer power rises where buyers are large, concentrated or face low switching costs. High forces overall compress margins, which shapes whether to enter, invest in or exit a market.","reforge":{"stem":"High barriers to entry in an industry most directly reduce:","options":{"A":"The bargaining power of existing buyers.","B":"Rivalry between established firms.","C":"The threat of new competitors entering","D":"The availability of substitute products."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-N3-09","stem":"Backward vertical integration involves a firm acquiring:","options":{"A":"A direct competitor at the same stage.","B":"A supplier earlier in its supply chain","C":"An unrelated business in another industry.","D":"A retailer that sells its finished goods."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Backward vertical integration acquires a supplier, securing input supply, quality and margin. Forward integration acquires a distributor or retailer, capturing the route to market and customer relationship. Horizontal integration acquires a competitor at the same stage, gaining share and scale. Conglomerate integration acquires an unrelated business, spreading risk but often lacking synergy and stretching management expertise.","reforge":{"stem":"A coffee chain buys the plantations that grow its beans. The main benefit sought is:","options":{"A":"Entry into an entirely unrelated market.","B":"Increased share of the retail coffee market.","C":"Access to a wider range of customers.","D":"Security of supply and control of quality"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-N3-10","stem":"Many mergers fail to deliver expected benefits mainly because of:","options":{"A":"An automatic loss of limited liability status.","B":"A legal requirement to reduce total output.","C":"Incompatible cultures and poor integration","D":"The impossibility of achieving any synergies."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Expected synergies — cost savings, cross-selling, shared expertise — frequently fail to appear because cultures clash, key staff leave, systems prove hard to integrate, management attention is diverted, and the acquirer overpays after optimistic forecasts. Successful integration needs early cultural due diligence, clear communication, retention of key people and realistic timescales. Organic growth is slower but avoids these risks.","reforge":{"stem":"Compared with acquisition, organic growth typically offers:","options":{"A":"Slower but lower-risk expansion","B":"Faster access to new markets and capacity.","C":"Immediate elimination of a competitor.","D":"Instant economies of scale on completion."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.2"},{"id":"BUS-N3-11","stem":"A business continuity plan is designed to ensure a firm can:","options":{"A":"Avoid paying insurance premiums on its assets.","B":"Guarantee that no disruption ever occurs.","C":"Eliminate all risk from its operations.","D":"Keep operating, or recover quickly"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Continuity planning identifies critical activities and the threats to them — fire, flood, cyber attack, supplier failure, key staff loss — then sets out preventive measures, backup arrangements, recovery priorities and communication. It cannot prevent every disruption; the aim is to limit downtime and damage. Related tools include risk assessment by likelihood and impact, insurance to transfer risk, and diversifying suppliers to reduce dependence.","reforge":{"stem":"Sourcing a critical component from two suppliers rather than one is an example of:","options":{"A":"Transferring the risk to an insurer.","B":"Reducing risk by avoiding dependence","C":"Accepting the risk without any action.","D":"Eliminating the risk of disruption entirely."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-12","stem":"A firm's corporate culture is best described as:","options":{"A":"The shared values and behaviours of its people","B":"The legal structure under which it is registered.","C":"The written contracts held with its employees.","D":"The formal organisational chart it publishes."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Culture is the shared values, attitudes and accepted behaviours that shape how work is actually done — 'the way we do things here'. Handy's types include power, role, task and person cultures. Culture affects motivation, risk-taking, innovation and how change is received; it is slow to shift because it rests on habits and beliefs rather than rules, which is why culture clash so often undermines mergers and restructuring.","reforge":{"stem":"Changing an established corporate culture is difficult mainly because it:","options":{"A":"Requires approval from the government regulator.","B":"Is fixed by the company's legal constitution.","C":"Rests on ingrained habits and shared beliefs","D":"Can only be altered by recruiting new directors."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-13","stem":"Kotter and Schlesinger identify a common cause of resistance to change as:","options":{"A":"Excessive consultation with the workforce.","B":"Fear of losing status, security or income","C":"Managers communicating the plan too clearly.","D":"Employees fully understanding the reasons."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"Resistance arises from self-interest, misunderstanding, low tolerance of change and genuine disagreement about its merits. Kotter and Schlesinger's responses range from education and communication, and participation, through facilitation and negotiation, to manipulation and coercion. The cooperative approaches take longer but produce more durable commitment; coercion is fast but breeds resentment and often fails once attention moves elsewhere.","reforge":{"stem":"Which approach to overcoming resistance is likely to produce the most durable commitment?","options":{"A":"Imposing the change by management authority.","B":"Offering a one-off payment to accept it.","C":"Announcing the change without explanation.","D":"Involving employees in planning the change"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-14","stem":"A firm becoming a flexible organisation is most likely to increase its use of:","options":{"A":"Permanent full-time contracts for all staff.","B":"A single rigid production process.","C":"Temporary, part-time and outsourced labour","D":"Long-term fixed supplier agreements only."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Flexibility can be numerical (varying headcount through temporary, part-time and agency staff), functional (multi-skilled staff moving between roles) or financial (performance-related pay). It lets a firm match capacity to fluctuating demand and control fixed costs, but can reduce job security, weaken commitment and loyalty, raise training needs, and risk losing accumulated expertise if too much work sits outside the permanent workforce.","reforge":{"stem":"A drawback of relying heavily on temporary staff is that the firm may experience:","options":{"A":"Weaker commitment and loss of expertise","B":"Reduced ability to vary capacity.","C":"An inability to recruit at short notice.","D":"Higher fixed costs during quiet periods."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-15","stem":"A firm's break-even output rises when:","options":{"A":"Selling price is increased significantly.","B":"Contribution per unit rises sharply.","C":"Variable cost per unit is reduced.","D":"Fixed costs rise, contribution flat"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even output = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit. It rises when fixed costs increase or when contribution falls, whether from a lower selling price or higher variable cost. It falls when fixed costs are cut, price is raised or variable costs are reduced. Reading this relationship correctly is what most break-even questions test: identify which term has moved and in which direction.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm raises its selling price with all costs unchanged. Its break-even output will:","options":{"A":"Rise, because revenue per unit is higher.","B":"Fall, because contribution per unit is higher","C":"Stay the same, since fixed costs are unchanged.","D":"Become impossible to calculate."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-2.2"},{"id":"BUS-N3-16","stem":"Research and development spending is best described as an investment because it:","options":{"A":"Incurs cost now for uncertain future returns","B":"Is treated as a variable cost of production.","C":"Guarantees a profitable new product will result.","D":"Is fully recovered within the same financial year."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"R&D consumes cash now against uncertain future benefit, so it pressures short-term profit while underpinning long-term competitiveness. Firms in fast-moving sectors such as pharmaceuticals and technology must spend heavily or fall behind; most projects fail, and returns depend on protecting the outcome through patents. Cutting R&D flatters this year's profit and is a common short-termism trap that weakens the firm later.","reforge":{"stem":"A patent supports a firm's return on research spending by:","options":{"A":"Guaranteeing demand for the new product.","B":"Reducing the cost of the research itself.","C":"Preventing rivals copying it","D":"Removing the need to market the product."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-17","stem":"Short-termism in a listed company is usually driven by:","options":{"A":"The absence of any published financial reporting.","B":"Pressure to deliver quick results to shareholders","C":"A legal requirement to reinvest all profits.","D":"Long-term incentive schemes for directors."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OWNERSHIP-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Quarterly reporting and share-price sensitivity push managers toward decisions that flatter near-term figures — cutting R&D, training or maintenance, deferring investment, or pursuing buybacks — at the expense of long-term capability. Counterweights include long-term incentive plans, patient or family ownership, private ownership away from public markets, and clear communication of strategy so investors judge progress on the right measures.","reforge":{"stem":"Which ownership structure is generally least exposed to short-term market pressure?","options":{"A":"A listed public limited company.","B":"A company with widely dispersed institutional shareholders.","C":"A firm preparing for a stock market flotation.","D":"A private family-owned business"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-18","stem":"A firm calculating its return on capital employed is measuring:","options":{"A":"Cash generated from day-to-day operations.","B":"The proportion of finance raised through debt.","C":"Operating profit over capital employed","D":"Revenue growth compared with the previous year."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-PROFIT-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"ROCE = operating profit ÷ capital employed × 100, showing how efficiently the finance invested in the business generates operating returns. It is the headline efficiency measure for comparing firms of different sizes and judging whether returns exceed the cost of capital. It should be read alongside margins, liquidity and gearing, and compared against trend and industry norms rather than a universal benchmark.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's ROCE falls while its operating profit is unchanged. This implies that:","options":{"A":"Capital employed has increased","B":"Its gross margin has necessarily declined.","C":"Its selling prices must have fallen.","D":"Its liquidity position has improved."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-19","stem":"A pressure group campaigning against a firm's environmental record is most likely to affect the firm through:","options":{"A":"A direct legal power to close its factories.","B":"Setting the rate of corporation tax it pays.","C":"Control over the firm's dividend policy.","D":"Reputational damage influencing consumers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Pressure groups lack formal authority but exert influence through publicity, consumer boycotts, lobbying for regulation, shareholder activism and media coverage. Their impact depends on how visible the brand is, how much consumers care, and how quickly the issue spreads. Firms respond by improving practice, engaging with the group, or reporting transparently — usually cheaper than defending a sustained campaign.","reforge":{"stem":"The most sustainable response to credible pressure group criticism is usually to:","options":{"A":"Increase advertising spending to drown it out.","B":"Address the practice being criticised","C":"Threaten legal action against the campaigners.","D":"Ignore the campaign until it subsides."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-N3-20","stem":"A firm adopting an ethical sourcing policy is most likely to face:","options":{"A":"Higher costs but stronger brand trust","B":"Lower input costs and higher volumes.","C":"Exemption from consumer protection law.","D":"A legal guarantee of increased sales."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Ethical sourcing — fair wages, safe conditions, environmental standards, no child labour — typically raises input costs and narrows the supplier pool, while requiring auditing. Benefits include reduced reputational and legal risk, access to ethically minded customers, better staff retention and more resilient supplier relationships. Whether it pays depends on whether the firm's customers value it enough to accept the price.","reforge":{"stem":"The business case for ethical sourcing is strongest when a firm's customers are:","options":{"A":"Buying purely on the lowest available price.","B":"Unaware of where the products originate.","C":"Willing to pay more for responsible sourcing","D":"Buying an undifferentiated commodity product."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-N3-21","stem":"Automation of a production process is most likely to raise:","options":{"A":"Variable costs per unit produced.","B":"Fixed costs, but lower labour cost","C":"The number of production employees needed.","D":"The firm's break-even output permanently."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Automation requires capital investment, raising fixed costs and depreciation, while cutting labour cost per unit and often improving consistency and speed. Break-even output usually rises initially because fixed costs are higher, so automation pays only at sufficient volume. Other consequences include redundancy costs and morale effects, a need for different technical skills, and reduced flexibility if the equipment is highly specialised.","reforge":{"stem":"Automation is least likely to be justified for a firm that:","options":{"A":"Produces very high volumes of a standard item.","B":"Faces rapidly rising wage costs.","C":"Requires highly consistent product quality.","D":"Produces small batches of varied bespoke items"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N3-22","stem":"Critical path analysis helps a project manager identify:","options":{"A":"The total profit the project will generate.","B":"The cheapest supplier for each material.","C":"Activities that cannot be delayed at all","D":"The number of employees the firm should recruit."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Critical path analysis maps activities, their durations and dependencies, giving the earliest start and latest finish times. The critical path is the longest sequence, with zero float, so any delay there delays the whole project. Float on other activities shows where slack exists and resources can be moved from. Limits: durations are estimates, the network needs updating as conditions change, and it says nothing about cost or quality.","reforge":{"stem":"An activity with two days of float means it can be delayed by two days without:","options":{"A":"Delaying the project's completion date","B":"Requiring any additional resources.","C":"Affecting the quality of the work done.","D":"Increasing the total cost of the project."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.1"}]},"BUS-4":{"label":"Theme 4 — Global Business","color":"#b45309","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","questions":[{"id":"BUS-T4-01","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"Globalisation refers to:","options":{"A":"increasing integration of economies, markets and businesses","B":"a country banning all imports","C":"Demand is perfectly inelastic — quantity did not respond at all to the price change.","D":"the removal of all exchange rates"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Globalisation links production, trade, finance and consumers across borders.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: Globalisation refers to:","options":{"A":"Prevents firms from investing overseas.","B":"Segmentation reduces the total size of the addressable market to a manageable level.","C":"increasing integration of economies, markets and businesses","D":"the removal of all exchange rates"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-02","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A tariff is:","options":{"A":"a subsidy paid to foreign consumers","B":"a tax on imported goods","C":"a limit on domestic production","D":"a floating exchange rate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Tariffs can protect domestic producers but raise import prices.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A tariff is:","options":{"A":"imports are always more expensive","B":"a limit on domestic production","C":"a floating exchange rate","D":"a tax on imported goods"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-03","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A quota restricts:","options":{"A":"the value of domestic wages","B":"Rising tariff barriers between major economies.","C":"the quantity of a product that may be imported","D":"the size of a firm's dividend"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Quotas limit volume rather than directly taxing each unit.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A quota restricts:","options":{"A":"the quantity of a product that may be imported","B":"Raises government revenue on each unit imported.","C":"the number of businesses in a country","D":"the size of a firm's dividend"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-04","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A multinational company operates:","options":{"A":"only through a government department","B":"with no overseas suppliers","C":"without any legal responsibilities","D":"in more than one country"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Multinationals coordinate activity across national markets.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A multinational company operates:","options":{"A":"Freedom from local regulation.","B":"in more than one country","C":"with no overseas suppliers","D":"without any legal responsibilities"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.4"},{"id":"BUS-T4-05","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"Foreign direct investment involves:","options":{"A":"investing in productive activity in another country","B":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market","C":"changing a domestic tax rate","D":"exporting without payment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"FDI can bring capital, jobs and technology, but may create dependence.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: Foreign direct investment involves:","options":{"A":"A firm borrowing from a domestic bank to finance local operations, without acquiring productive assets abroad.","B":"changing a domestic tax rate","C":"investing in productive activity in another country","D":"exporting without payment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-T4-06","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A depreciation of sterling tends to make UK exports:","options":{"A":"more expensive in every overseas market","B":"cheaper in foreign-currency terms","C":"unavailable to foreign buyers","D":"unaffected by price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"The effect can raise competitiveness, although imported inputs become dearer.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: A depreciation of sterling tends to make UK exports:","options":{"A":"illegal to purchase","B":"Reducing the business's tax liability","C":"unaffected by price","D":"cheaper in foreign-currency terms"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-07","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"If sterling appreciates, imported components generally become:","options":{"A":"more expensive in every case","B":"illegal to purchase","C":"cheaper in sterling terms","D":"unrelated to exchange rates"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"An appreciation increases the foreign purchasing power of sterling.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: If sterling appreciates, imported components generally become:","options":{"A":"cheaper in sterling terms","B":"A subsidy paid to domestic producers.","C":"illegal to purchase","D":"unrelated to exchange rates"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-08","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A current account deficit means:","options":{"A":"Highly geared firms have more employees to pay, increasing fixed labour costs.","B":"exports exceed imports","C":"the exchange rate is fixed","D":"outflows on trade, income and transfers exceed inflows"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"The current account records trade plus primary and secondary income flows.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A current account deficit means:","options":{"A":"A fiscal deficit caused by government spending exceeding tax revenue, rather than a deficit on trade, income and transfers.","B":"outflows on trade, income and transfers exceed inflows","C":"exports exceed imports","D":"the exchange rate is fixed"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-09","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A benefit of outsourcing production abroad may be:","options":{"A":"lower unit costs or access to specialist suppliers","B":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market","C":"no transport or coordination costs","D":"guaranteed quality in every case"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Outsourcing can reduce costs but creates supply-chain and reputational risks.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A benefit of outsourcing production abroad may be:","options":{"A":"The exact behaviour of every competitor, which a business cannot guarantee when it outsources production abroad.","B":"no transport or coordination costs","C":"lower unit costs or access to specialist suppliers","D":"guaranteed quality in every case"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-T4-10","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"An ethical concern about global supply chains is:","options":{"A":"Regulators will exempt the firm from inspection.","B":"poor labour conditions in supplier factories","C":"the use of accounting ratios","D":"a fall in local advertising"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Firms may face pressure to monitor wages, safety and working hours.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: An ethical concern about global supply chains is:","options":{"A":"A central bank reserve requirement, a monetary-policy detail unrelated to ethical concerns raised by global supply chains.","B":"the use of accounting ratios","C":"a fall in local advertising","D":"poor labour conditions in supplier factories"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-11","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A trade bloc is:","options":{"A":"Segmentation is designed to guarantee that every possible customer purchases the product.","B":"a restriction on all exports","C":"a group of countries agreeing to reduce trade barriers between members","D":"a central bank interest-rate committee"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Trade blocs can increase trade within the group but may divert trade from outsiders.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A trade bloc is:","options":{"A":"a group of countries agreeing to reduce trade barriers between members","B":"Both 40% — gross and operating margins are always identical by accounting definition.","C":"a restriction on all exports","D":"a central bank interest-rate committee"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-T4-12","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"Protectionism is designed primarily to:","options":{"A":"remove every domestic subsidy","B":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market","C":"prevent firms from exporting","D":"protect domestic industries from foreign competition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Protectionist measures include tariffs, quotas and subsidies.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: Protectionism is designed primarily to:","options":{"A":"imports are always more expensive for every overseas firm","B":"protect domestic industries from foreign competition","C":"increase import competition without limits","D":"prevent firms from exporting"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-13","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A subsidy to domestic exporters may:","options":{"A":"lower their costs and improve international competitiveness","B":"raise their costs automatically","C":"Gross 60%, operating 24% — calculated by dividing each by total costs.","D":"make imports illegal"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Subsidies can support exports but may provoke retaliation and fiscal costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A subsidy to domestic exporters may:","options":{"A":"Its own export prices rise abroad.","B":"Highly geared firms always operate with permanently lower profit margins.","C":"lower their costs and improve international competitiveness","D":"make imports illegal"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-14","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A global marketing strategy may need adaptation because:","options":{"A":"all consumers have identical preferences","B":"cultures, incomes and regulations differ between markets","C":"exchange rates never change","D":"imports are always more expensive for every overseas firm"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Standardisation saves costs, while adaptation can improve local relevance.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A global marketing strategy may need adaptation because:","options":{"A":"all countries use different currencies","B":"exchange rates never change","C":"A central bank reserve requirement, a monetary-policy detail unrelated to why a global marketing strategy may need local adaptation.","D":"cultures, incomes and regulations differ between markets"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.3"},{"id":"BUS-T4-15","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A remittance is:","options":{"A":"a tax on imports","B":"a firm's retained profit","C":"money sent by a worker to people in another country","D":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Remittances can be important income for households and developing economies.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A remittance is:","options":{"A":"money sent by a worker to people in another country","B":"Guaranteeing no costs in the short term, an outcome unrelated to what a remittance actually is.","C":"a firm's retained profit","D":"a central bank reserve requirement"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4-16","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"An emerging market is generally characterised by:","options":{"A":"zero economic growth","B":"no financial markets","C":"Segmentation is a legal requirement for all consumer-facing firms under UK marketing law.","D":"rapid growth and increasing integration but developing institutions or income levels"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Emerging markets can offer growth but also greater political and currency risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: An emerging market is generally characterised by:","options":{"A":"A central bank reserve requirement, a monetary-policy detail unrelated to what generally characterises an emerging market.","B":"rapid growth and increasing integration but developing institutions or income levels","C":"no financial markets","D":"complete isolation from global trade"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-T4-17","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A business may hedge currency risk by:","options":{"A":"using a financial contract to lock in or protect an exchange rate","B":"ignoring all overseas invoices","C":"stopping every export","D":"Exports become more expensive for foreign buyers, reducing demand."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Hedging reduces uncertainty but may limit gains if exchange rates move favourably.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A business may hedge currency risk by:","options":{"A":"Interest rates automatically fall.","B":"stopping every export","C":"using a financial contract to lock in or protect an exchange rate","D":"Exchange rate movements have no effect on exporters who invoice in sterling."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-01","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A free-trade agreement generally aims to:","options":{"A":"Must fund every new outlet from its own capital.","B":"reduce barriers between participating countries","C":"set one global wage","D":"replace every domestic tax"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Reducing barriers can increase trade, although firms may face stronger competition.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: A free-trade agreement generally aims to:","options":{"A":"stopping every export","B":"set one global wage","C":"Raises government revenue on each unit imported.","D":"reduce barriers between participating countries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-02","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A multinational may transfer profits between countries partly because:","options":{"A":"preventing all disagreements between stakeholders","B":"exchange rates cannot change","C":"tax rules and rates differ between jurisdictions","D":"customers cannot compare prices"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Tax planning can affect location decisions, but avoidance and evasion are different and laws apply.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: A multinational may transfer profits between countries partly because:","options":{"A":"tax rules and rates differ between jurisdictions","B":"Replaces the need for audited financial accounts.","C":"exchange rates cannot change","D":"Guaranteeing no costs in the short term, an outcome no genuine transfer-pricing decision could actually deliver for a multinational."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-03","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A supply-chain disruption is most likely to expose dependence on:","options":{"A":"All consumers have identical preferences, an assumption unrelated to what a supply-chain disruption is most likely to expose.","B":"large domestic inventories","C":"flexible local production","D":"a small number of overseas suppliers or routes"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Concentration can lower costs but makes disruption more damaging.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A supply-chain disruption is most likely to expose dependence on:","options":{"A":"A complete absence of overseas suppliers or routes, because disruption exposes concentration rather than independence.","B":"a small number of overseas suppliers or routes","C":"large domestic inventories","D":"flexible local production"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-04","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A country's balance of trade records:","options":{"A":"the value of exports and imports of goods","B":"capital inflows only","C":"Lower corporation tax in the home country.","D":"all government borrowing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"The goods balance is one part of the wider current account.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: A country's balance of trade records:","options":{"A":"Governments restricting foreign investment.","B":"household saving and investment","C":"the value of exports and imports of goods","D":"all government borrowing"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-05","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A trade surplus occurs when:","options":{"A":"imports exceed exports","B":"the value of exports exceeds the value of imports","C":"tax revenue exceeds spending","D":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"A surplus on goods or services indicates export value is greater than import value for that category.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: A trade surplus occurs when:","options":{"A":"imports are always more expensive for every overseas firm","B":"tax revenue exceeds spending","C":"capital outflows exceed inflows","D":"the value of exports exceeds the value of imports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-06","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A firm's country risk includes the possibility of:","options":{"A":"QA inspectors receive lower wages than QC inspectors at every level.","B":"perfectly stable regulation","C":"political or economic events disrupting overseas operations","D":"zero exchange-rate movement"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Country risk can include instability, policy change, restrictions and weak infrastructure.","reforge":{"stem":"For an applied business question: A firm's country risk includes the possibility of:","options":{"A":"political or economic events disrupting overseas operations","B":"The increase in average costs as a firm grows beyond its optimal size.","C":"Guaranteeing no costs in the short term, an outcome no assessment of a firm's country risk could actually deliver.","D":"zero exchange-rate movement"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-07","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A competitive advantage based on low wages may be weakened by:","options":{"A":"The total reduction in fixed costs when a firm closes a factory.","B":"greater brand loyalty","C":"more efficient logistics","D":"rising wages, automation or stronger productivity elsewhere"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Cost advantages change when productivity, technology and wages change.","reforge":{"stem":"A business case presents this situation: A competitive advantage based on low wages may be weakened by:","options":{"A":"The benefit of producing in a single location rather than multiple sites.","B":"rising wages, automation or stronger productivity elsewhere","C":"greater brand loyalty","D":"Guaranteeing no costs in the short term, an outcome unrelated to why a low-wage competitive advantage may weaken over time."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-T4B-08","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"Corporate social responsibility may improve long-term performance by:","options":{"A":"building trust with customers, workers and communities","B":"removing the need for regulation","C":"imports are always more expensive for every overseas firm","D":"guaranteeing no costs in the short term"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"CSR can support reputation and relationships, although it may involve short-term costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A manager applies the principle to: Corporate social responsibility may improve long-term performance by:","options":{"A":"Is prepared only for the home market.","B":"Price skimming — high initial price for early adopters.","C":"building trust with customers, workers and communities","D":"Guaranteeing no costs in the short term, an outcome unrelated to why corporate social responsibility may improve long-term performance."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-GLOBAL-01","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A business expanding into an emerging market should first consider:","options":{"A":"only the size of the country's land area","B":"local demand, infrastructure, regulation and country risk","C":"Financial economies — the bank offers better interest rates.","D":"the firm's domestic logo colour"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Expansion decisions should assess market conditions, institutional constraints and operational risk.","reforge":{"stem":"In a company planning example: A business expanding into an emerging market should first consider:","options":{"A":"imports are always more expensive","B":"The UK exporter must raise prices to maintain profit margins.","C":"the firm's domestic logo colour","D":"local demand, infrastructure, regulation and country risk"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-GLOBAL-02","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"Foreign direct investment involves: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The manufacturer must reduce sterling prices to maintain market share.","B":"borrowing only from a local bank","C":"establishing or acquiring productive operations in another country","D":"reducing the number of overseas suppliers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"FDI gives a business a lasting interest in productive activity abroad.","reforge":{"stem":"An entrepreneur encounters this decision: Foreign direct investment involves: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"establishing or acquiring productive operations in another country","B":"Product orientation focuses on price; market orientation focuses on quality.","C":"borrowing only from a local bank","D":"reducing the number of overseas suppliers"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-N4-01","stem":"Globalisation has increased trade partly because of:","options":{"A":"Rising tariff barriers between major economies.","B":"Governments restricting foreign investment.","C":"A worldwide decline in containerised shipping.","D":"Falling transport and communication costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Drivers of globalisation include containerisation and cheaper transport, digital communication, trade liberalisation through the WTO and trade blocs, deregulation of capital movements, and the growth of multinationals. Consequences: larger markets, access to cheaper inputs, greater competition, and interdependence that transmits shocks quickly. Criticisms centre on labour standards, environmental cost and uneven distribution of gains.","reforge":{"stem":"A consequence of greater global interdependence is that a firm becomes more exposed to:","options":{"A":"Domestic competition only.","B":"Economic shocks originating overseas","C":"Changes in its local council's policy.","D":"Reduced choice of potential suppliers."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-02","stem":"A tariff imposed on imported goods is:","options":{"A":"A tax that raises the price of imports","B":"A physical limit on the quantity imported.","C":"A subsidy paid to domestic producers.","D":"A standard that imported goods must meet."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Protectionist measures: tariffs are taxes raising import prices; quotas physically limit quantities; subsidies lower domestic producers' costs; and non-tariff barriers use standards, licensing and administrative requirements. They protect domestic jobs and infant industries in the short run, but raise consumer prices, reduce choice, invite retaliation and shelter inefficiency. Exporting firms face them as a direct barrier to market entry.","reforge":{"stem":"A quota differs from a tariff because a quota:","options":{"A":"Raises government revenue on each unit imported.","B":"Applies only to services rather than goods.","C":"Limits the quantity that may be imported","D":"Lowers the price paid by domestic consumers."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-03","stem":"A firm choosing to enter a foreign market through a joint venture rather than direct investment gains:","options":{"A":"Complete control over all strategic decisions.","B":"Local knowledge and shared risk","C":"The whole of any profit generated.","D":"Freedom from local regulation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Entry methods trade control against risk. Exporting is lowest cost and risk but distant from the customer. Licensing and franchising use local partners' capital and knowledge for a fee or royalty, with some loss of quality control. Joint ventures share cost, risk and local expertise but also profit and control, and can suffer partner disputes. Wholly owned direct investment gives full control and profit at the highest cost and risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A common difficulty with international joint ventures is:","options":{"A":"The absence of any local market knowledge.","B":"Being unable to share the capital cost.","C":"Full exposure to all of the investment risk.","D":"Disagreement between partners over strategy"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-04","stem":"Offshoring differs from outsourcing because offshoring specifically involves:","options":{"A":"Handing an activity to an external provider.","B":"Returning production to the home country.","C":"Relocating an activity to another country","D":"Selling a business division to a competitor."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Outsourcing means using an external provider, which may be domestic. Offshoring means moving the activity abroad, whether to a third party or the firm's own subsidiary. Motives are lower labour costs, skills availability, proximity to markets and favourable tax or regulation. Risks include quality control, longer and more fragile supply chains, cultural and time-zone friction, currency exposure and reputational scrutiny of labour standards.","reforge":{"stem":"Reshoring production to the home country is most often motivated by:","options":{"A":"Rising overseas costs and risk","B":"A desire to reduce domestic employment.","C":"Lower quality standards at home.","D":"Increased tariffs on domestic sales."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-N4-05","stem":"A multinational is most likely to standardise its product globally when:","options":{"A":"Local tastes differ sharply between markets.","B":"Each country has distinct legal requirements.","C":"Distribution channels vary widely by country.","D":"Consumer needs are similar across markets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Standardisation — one product and message worldwide — captures economies of scale, consistent branding and lower development cost, and suits globally similar needs, as in technology and luxury goods. Adaptation (glocalisation) tailors product, promotion or packaging to local tastes, language, regulation and buying power. Most firms sit between the two, keeping a global brand while adapting flavours, sizes, pricing or advertising locally.","reforge":{"stem":"A fast-food chain selling different menu items in India than in the UK is practising:","options":{"A":"Full standardisation of its global offer.","B":"Adaptation of the product to local tastes","C":"Offshoring of its production facilities.","D":"Vertical integration of its supply chain."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-06","stem":"Hofstede's model of national culture is useful to a multinational because it:","options":{"A":"Differences affecting management","B":"Sets the tariff rate applying in each market.","C":"Measures the size of each national market.","D":"Predicts exchange rate movements accurately."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Hofstede compares national cultures on dimensions including power distance, individualism versus collectivism, uncertainty avoidance, masculinity versus femininity, and long-term orientation. It helps anticipate how management style, negotiation, incentives, hierarchy and marketing messages may need to differ between countries. Its limitations are that it describes averages, can encourage stereotyping, and rests on data that ages as societies change.","reforge":{"stem":"A significant limitation of using Hofstede's dimensions is that they:","options":{"A":"Change unpredictably from month to month.","B":"Apply only to firms in the service sector.","C":"Describe averages, risking stereotypes","D":"Cannot be compared between two countries."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-07","stem":"An emerging economy is typically attractive to a multinational because of its:","options":{"A":"Fully mature and saturated consumer market.","B":"Rapid growth and rising consumer incomes","C":"Highly restrictive limits on foreign ownership.","D":"Established and unchanging market shares."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Emerging economies offer fast GDP growth, a growing middle class, urbanisation, and often lower production costs — attractive when home markets are saturated. Risks include political instability, weaker legal protection for contracts and intellectual property, currency volatility, infrastructure gaps, and unfamiliar regulation. Firms manage these through joint ventures with local partners, staged entry and careful political risk assessment.","reforge":{"stem":"Which risk is most specific to operating in a less politically stable emerging market?","options":{"A":"Competition from other international firms.","B":"The need to adapt products to local tastes.","C":"Rising labour costs as the economy grows.","D":"Weak enforcement of contract rights"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-08","stem":"A firm's international competitiveness is improved most directly by:","options":{"A":"Raising its prices above those of rivals.","B":"Increasing the length of its supply chain.","C":"Raising productivity against wage costs","D":"Reducing its spending on staff training."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Price competitiveness depends on unit labour costs — wages relative to productivity — plus exchange rates, input costs and scale. Non-price competitiveness rests on quality, design, brand, reliability, service and delivery speed, and is generally more durable because it is harder to copy and less exposed to currency swings. Sustained investment in skills, technology and innovation underpins both.","reforge":{"stem":"Non-price competitiveness is often more sustainable than price competitiveness because it is:","options":{"A":"Harder for rivals to imitate quickly","B":"Guaranteed by international trade agreements.","C":"Unaffected by changes in consumer taste.","D":"Cheaper to achieve in the short term."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.5"},{"id":"BUS-N4-09","stem":"A trade bloc such as the EU single market primarily benefits member firms by:","options":{"A":"Fixing exchange rates against all currencies.","B":"Guaranteeing identical prices in every country.","C":"Eliminating competition within the bloc.","D":"Removing barriers to trade between members"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"A trade bloc removes tariffs and quotas between members and harmonises standards, enlarging the accessible market and enabling economies of scale. Costs: a common external tariff can raise input prices from outside the bloc, competition within it intensifies, and members accept shared rules limiting national policy. Trade creation refers to new trade between members; trade diversion to trade shifted away from cheaper non-members.","reforge":{"stem":"Trade diversion occurs when a trade bloc causes members to buy from:","options":{"A":"The cheapest available global supplier.","B":"A less efficient member of the bloc","C":"Domestic suppliers only, ignoring all imports.","D":"Suppliers chosen at random within the bloc."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-N4-10","stem":"A UK firm importing components priced in US dollars faces exchange rate risk because:","options":{"A":"A weaker pound raises the cost in sterling","B":"Tariffs on components change every quarter.","C":"Its selling prices are fixed by regulation.","D":"Its suppliers must accept payment in sterling."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Transaction risk arises where payments or receipts are in foreign currency and rates move between agreeing and settling. Firms manage it by hedging with forward contracts fixing a future rate, invoicing in their own currency, or matching foreign currency revenues against costs — a natural hedge. Hedging brings certainty for budgeting but has a cost and removes any benefit from a favourable move.","reforge":{"stem":"A forward exchange contract helps a firm mainly by:","options":{"A":"Guaranteeing a profit on the transaction.","B":"Eliminating the need to pay the supplier.","C":"Fixing the rate, making costs predictable","D":"Allowing it to benefit from every rate move."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-11","stem":"A multinational accused of paying very low wages overseas typically defends this by arguing that wages are:","options":{"A":"Set entirely by the firm's own shareholders.","B":"Above the local rate and minimum","C":"Identical to those paid in its home country.","D":"Unrelated to local living costs entirely."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Multinationals argue they pay at or above local rates and legal minima, create employment and transfer skills. Critics respond that legal minima may be below a living wage, that comparison with the home country reveals the gap, and that competitive pressure drives a race to the bottom. Reputational exposure is greatest for consumer-facing brands, which is why many now publish supplier audits and set standards above local law.","reforge":{"stem":"Consumer-facing brands face greater pressure over overseas labour standards mainly because:","options":{"A":"They are subject to different employment law.","B":"Their supply chains are always shorter.","C":"They pay higher rates of corporation tax.","D":"Their reputation directly affects sales"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-12","stem":"Transfer pricing allows a multinational to:","options":{"A":"Fix the retail price charged in every country.","B":"Avoid all regulation of its overseas operations.","C":"Set prices between its own subsidiaries","D":"Guarantee identical profit margins worldwide."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Transfer pricing sets the price at which one subsidiary sells to another. Because it shifts where profit is recorded, it can move profit toward low-tax jurisdictions. It is legal where prices reflect genuine arm's-length value, but aggressive use attracts regulatory challenge, back-tax demands and reputational damage. Tax authorities increasingly require documentation justifying that intra-group prices match market rates.","reforge":{"stem":"Aggressive transfer pricing exposes a multinational chiefly to:","options":{"A":"Reputational and tax challenge","B":"A legal ban on trading internationally.","C":"An automatic rise in its labour costs.","D":"Loss of limited liability for its directors."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-13","stem":"A firm's decision to locate production near its target market rather than at lowest cost is most likely driven by:","options":{"A":"A wish to increase total transport distance.","B":"The availability of cheaper overseas labour.","C":"Lower corporation tax in the home country.","D":"Shorter lead times, less transport"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Location decisions weigh labour cost and availability, proximity to markets and suppliers, transport and infrastructure, government incentives and tariffs, exchange rates, and political stability. Producing near the market cuts lead times and shipping cost, eases responsiveness and may avoid tariffs, but may raise labour cost. The right balance depends on how bulky the product is, how fast demand changes and how price sensitive customers are.","reforge":{"stem":"Producing close to the target market is most valuable for goods that are:","options":{"A":"Small, light and non-perishable.","B":"Bulky, with fast-changing demand","C":"Stored for several years before sale.","D":"Sold at identical prices worldwide."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-1.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-14","stem":"Foreign direct investment differs from portfolio investment because FDI involves:","options":{"A":"A lasting interest and control abroad","B":"Lending money to an overseas government.","C":"Buying shares purely for financial return.","D":"Exporting finished goods to another country."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"FDI establishes a lasting interest and management influence in an overseas enterprise, through building facilities, acquisition or a controlling stake. Portfolio investment buys financial assets for return without control and can be withdrawn quickly. Host countries generally prefer FDI because it brings jobs, skills, technology and tax revenue and is less volatile, though it raises concerns about foreign ownership of strategic assets.","reforge":{"stem":"Host governments often prefer FDI to portfolio investment because FDI is:","options":{"A":"Exempt from all forms of local taxation.","B":"Guaranteed to be more profitable.","C":"Less easily withdrawn at short notice","D":"Restricted to the financial services sector."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.2"},{"id":"BUS-N4-15","stem":"A firm exporting for the first time is most likely to reduce its risk by:","options":{"A":"Investing immediately in an overseas factory.","B":"Using an agent or distributor in the market","C":"Acquiring a competitor in the target country.","D":"Setting up a wholly owned foreign subsidiary."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Staged internationalisation limits exposure: begin by exporting through an agent or distributor who already knows the market's customers, regulation and channels, then deepen commitment as knowledge grows — a sales office, then a joint venture, then wholly owned operations. Each step raises control and potential return alongside cost and risk. Committing heavily before understanding the market is a common and expensive error.","reforge":{"stem":"As a firm moves from exporting through agents to a wholly owned subsidiary, it gains control but also:","options":{"A":"Loses access to the overseas market.","B":"Reduces its total capital investment.","C":"Removes all exchange rate exposure.","D":"Takes on greater cost and risk"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-16","stem":"A weaker domestic currency helps a firm competing against imports because:","options":{"A":"Its imported raw materials become cheaper.","B":"Its own export prices rise abroad.","C":"Imported rival products become dearer","D":"Interest rates automatically fall."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-INTEREST-CURRENCY","scaffold":"A weaker currency raises the domestic price of imports, helping firms that compete with them, and lowers the foreign-currency price of exports, helping exporters. It simultaneously raises the cost of imported inputs, so a firm reliant on foreign components is squeezed. The overall effect on any given firm depends on the balance between its foreign currency costs and revenues — matching the two is a natural hedge.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm with foreign currency revenues roughly equal to its foreign currency costs has:","options":{"A":"A natural hedge against currency movements","B":"Maximum exposure to exchange rate movements.","C":"No need to trade internationally at all.","D":"A guaranteed profit on overseas sales."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-17","stem":"The main argument that globalisation harms some developed-economy workers is that it:","options":{"A":"Increases the price of imported consumer goods.","B":"Prevents firms from investing overseas.","C":"Reduces the total size of the world market.","D":"Shifts lower-skilled work abroad"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Globalisation lowers consumer prices, widens choice and raises incomes in many developing economies, but relocation of lower-skilled production can displace workers in developed economies, with losses concentrated in particular regions and industries while gains are spread thinly. Policy responses include retraining, regional investment and education, since the aggregate gain does not automatically reach those who bear the cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A common policy response to job losses caused by offshoring is:","options":{"A":"Banning all imports from lower-cost economies.","B":"Retraining and regional investment programmes","C":"Fixing the exchange rate permanently.","D":"Requiring firms to raise their prices."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-18","stem":"A global brand gains most of its value from:","options":{"A":"Consistent recognition and trust across markets","B":"The physical assets recorded on its balance sheet.","C":"The number of countries it holds patents in.","D":"Its total spending on advertising last year."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-PRODUCT-GROWTH","scaffold":"A global brand delivers consistent recognition, trust and meaning across borders, supporting premium pricing, cheaper market entry for new products and scale economies in promotion. It requires consistent quality and message worldwide, so a failure in one market can damage the brand everywhere — which is why multinationals monitor supplier conduct and product safety globally rather than market by market.","reforge":{"stem":"A quality failure in one overseas market threatens a global brand because reputation is:","options":{"A":"Protected by international trademark law.","B":"Measured separately in each country.","C":"Shared across all its markets","D":"Recorded as an asset in the accounts."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-19","stem":"A firm assessing whether to enter a new country would use PESTLE analysis to examine:","options":{"A":"Its own internal strengths and weaknesses.","B":"External political and social factors","C":"The contribution earned per unit sold.","D":"The float available on each project activity."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-STRATEGY-DECISIONS","scaffold":"PESTLE covers Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal and Environmental factors — all external to the firm. It is used to scan the operating environment before entering a market or planning strategy, and complements SWOT, which combines internal strengths and weaknesses with external opportunities and threats. The value lies in identifying which factors materially affect this firm, rather than listing every possible influence.","reforge":{"stem":"An ageing population in a target market would be recorded under PESTLE as:","options":{"A":"A legal factor requiring compliance.","B":"A political factor set by government.","C":"A technological factor changing production.","D":"A social factor affecting demand patterns"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-20","stem":"A firm's decision to franchise internationally rather than open its own outlets means it:","options":{"A":"Retains full control of daily operations.","B":"Must fund every new outlet from its own capital.","C":"Expands using franchisees' capital","D":"Takes on all the operating risk in each market."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Franchising lets a firm expand quickly using franchisees' capital and local knowledge while earning fees and royalties, with limited capital risk of its own. The trade-off is reduced control: inconsistent standards at one franchise can damage the brand everywhere, so franchisors invest heavily in training, specification and monitoring. Disputes over territory, fees and standards are common and contracts must anticipate them.","reforge":{"stem":"The principal risk a franchisor accepts when expanding through franchising is:","options":{"A":"Inconsistent standards harming it","B":"Providing all the capital for expansion.","C":"Losing the right to earn any royalties.","D":"Being unable to enter foreign markets."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"BUS-N4-21","stem":"Corporate social responsibility reporting is most valuable to a multinational when it:","options":{"A":"Replaces the need for audited financial accounts.","B":"Is prepared only for the home market.","C":"Focuses solely on charitable donations.","D":"Reports verifiable performance against targets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBUS-ETHICS-RISK","scaffold":"Credible CSR reporting sets measurable targets — emissions, waste, supplier audits, safety, diversity — and reports verified progress, including shortfalls. It supports reputation, investor confidence and staff recruitment, and increasingly meets regulatory expectations. Reporting that lists donations without measurable operational change invites accusations of greenwashing, which does more reputational harm than not reporting at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Greenwashing describes a firm that:","options":{"A":"Invests heavily in reducing emissions.","B":"Overstates its environmental performance","C":"Publishes independently audited targets.","D":"Reports honestly on missed objectives."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-3.4"},{"id":"BUS-N4-22","stem":"A firm's supply chain becomes more vulnerable as it globalises mainly because:","options":{"A":"Longer chains have more points of failure","B":"Transport costs fall with longer distances.","C":"Suppliers become easier to inspect in person.","D":"Fewer suppliers exist in the world market."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBUS-OPERATIONS","scaffold":"Global supply chains add distance, intermediaries, border formalities and currency exposure, so there are more points at which disruption can occur — port closures, weather, political action, supplier failure — and lead times are longer, making recovery slower. Resilience measures include dual sourcing, holding strategic buffer stock, regional rather than single global sourcing, and mapping the chain beyond immediate first-tier suppliers.","reforge":{"stem":"Mapping suppliers beyond the first tier helps a firm because disruption often begins:","options":{"A":"Only within the firm's own factories.","B":"At the retailer selling to consumers.","C":"Deeper in the chain","D":"With the customer's payment terms."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BUS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.1"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-BUS4-01","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"An international brand keeps its logo worldwide but changes its advertising to fit local customs. This is:","options":{"A":"a tariff on imports","B":"adapted global marketing","C":"vertical integration","D":"a country banning all imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Global firms often standardise core identity while adapting promotion to local culture.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm tests the idea in practice: An international brand keeps its logo worldwide but changes its advertising to fit local customs. This is:","options":{"A":"Reducing its spending on staff training.","B":"adapted global marketing","C":"vertical integration","D":"a fixed exchange rate"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.3"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-BUS4-02","spec":"A LEVEL BUSINESS","stem":"A company entering a new overseas market should localise promotion mainly because:","options":{"A":"Market orientation only applies to services, not manufactured goods.","B":"imports are always more expensive","C":"consumer preferences and meanings can vary between cultures","D":"local firms cannot advertise online"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBUS-GLOBAL-TRADE","scaffold":"Cultural expectations can change how consumers interpret a message or product.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm adapts its overseas promotion for local audiences. What is the strongest reason?","options":{"A":"exchange rates are identical in every overseas market","B":"consumer preferences and meanings can vary between cultures","C":"There is no practical difference between the two approaches.","D":"local businesses cannot use digital advertising"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-bus-4.3"}]}});
